})}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e81aa8f-4f0c-580e-8d47-2566380e9716"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e81aa8f-4f0c-580e-8d47-2566380e9716" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e81aa8f-4f0c-580e-8d47-2566380e9716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a507fac6-cc7c-5ccd-80d1-92dffbbe2f70" "answer-2eb956e4-324e-5b0c-8781-18bffb7582ae" "answer-eaf1341a-cc4c-5d47-be72-ffec29422a3c" "answer-f7f53762-c988-521c-bc03-a77b4ff640a9" "answer-0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a507fac6-cc7c-5ccd-80d1-92dffbbe2f70" :answer/id #uuid "a507fac6-cc7c-5ccd-80d1-92dffbbe2f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eb956e4-324e-5b0c-8781-18bffb7582ae" :answer/id #uuid "2eb956e4-324e-5b0c-8781-18bffb7582ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaf1341a-cc4c-5d47-be72-ffec29422a3c" :answer/id #uuid "eaf1341a-cc4c-5d47-be72-ffec29422a3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{12}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7f53762-c988-521c-bc03-a77b4ff640a9" :answer/id #uuid "f7f53762-c988-521c-bc03-a77b4ff640a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{6}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa" :answer/id #uuid "0321eccf-a272-5f41-9dc6-3afd94e414aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{6}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77363ffd-e93f-5e69-9243-0a270550e6fc" :question/id #uuid "77363ffd-e93f-5e69-9243-0a270550e6fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-100392" :question/problem "${({9}^{3})}^{-6}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({9}^{3})}^{-6}={9}^{3\\cdot (-6)}={9}^{-18}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{-18},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(9)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{9}^{-18}={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de337785-0bed-5d88-927f-c04df51fff3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de337785-0bed-5d88-927f-c04df51fff3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "de337785-0bed-5d88-927f-c04df51fff3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" "answer-2150377d-9170-5e26-8c05-6932a47baf31" "answer-78a5e0b0-0aff-56f5-b302-e96894a6e57c" "answer-01c03964-bbb3-565c-bfcb-c6ed69ba5e55" "answer-8ff6cccd-26e2-5560-99d7-944b84007b58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" :answer/id #uuid "96a52254-ea30-53d5-b2d5-8fa3a1d84466" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2150377d-9170-5e26-8c05-6932a47baf31" :answer/id #uuid "2150377d-9170-5e26-8c05-6932a47baf31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78a5e0b0-0aff-56f5-b302-e96894a6e57c" :answer/id #uuid "78a5e0b0-0aff-56f5-b302-e96894a6e57c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01c03964-bbb3-565c-bfcb-c6ed69ba5e55" :answer/id #uuid "01c03964-bbb3-565c-bfcb-c6ed69ba5e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{9}^{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ff6cccd-26e2-5560-99d7-944b84007b58" :answer/id #uuid "8ff6cccd-26e2-5560-99d7-944b84007b58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{9}^{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101969 :tutorial/content "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ {{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101076 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186123212 :answer-field/choices 17592186123217] [:db/retract 17592186123212 :answer-field/choices 17592186123213] [:db/retract 17592186123212 :answer-field/choices 17592186123214] [:db/retract 17592186123212 :answer-field/choices 17592186123215] {:db/id 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f2c9542-25be-572a-a669-8d12401722e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125102 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 11 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 11 & 8-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )+20) & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123212 :answer-field/correct 17592186123216 :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b774372-51ae-5bda-8eff-29ba0b1a454a" "answer-45c01a88-e97e-59fa-b369-0c03e469bc6a" "answer-6757aa5b-9c37-5abb-9da9-6dbe52e7e8f9" "answer-8a24d661-bdd6-5639-9e9f-991e9c96a769"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b774372-51ae-5bda-8eff-29ba0b1a454a" :answer/id #uuid "2b774372-51ae-5bda-8eff-29ba0b1a454a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c01a88-e97e-59fa-b369-0c03e469bc6a" :answer/id #uuid "45c01a88-e97e-59fa-b369-0c03e469bc6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6757aa5b-9c37-5abb-9da9-6dbe52e7e8f9" :answer/id #uuid "6757aa5b-9c37-5abb-9da9-6dbe52e7e8f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a24d661-bdd6-5639-9e9f-991e9c96a769" :answer/id #uuid "8a24d661-bdd6-5639-9e9f-991e9c96a769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f2c9542-25be-572a-a669-8d12401722e1" :question/id #uuid "7f2c9542-25be-572a-a669-8d12401722e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84600" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+24) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-95c98126-2281-5305-93e6-da18146facd3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-95c98126-2281-5305-93e6-da18146facd3" :answer-field/id #uuid "95c98126-2281-5305-93e6-da18146facd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9666dbcc-708b-575b-a68d-ee485b61f0dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e3287fd-4f76-531c-8093-5fc460eaebc3" "answer-3ff48332-0b7f-5b0d-8590-a4d156a2b875" "answer-949111d0-6bc2-5d15-8315-e4a2fc30e721" "answer-69523ed9-1689-5400-b80a-645cd83eb2ce" "answer-9666dbcc-708b-575b-a68d-ee485b61f0dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3287fd-4f76-531c-8093-5fc460eaebc3" :answer/id #uuid "8e3287fd-4f76-531c-8093-5fc460eaebc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ff48332-0b7f-5b0d-8590-a4d156a2b875" :answer/id #uuid "3ff48332-0b7f-5b0d-8590-a4d156a2b875" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-949111d0-6bc2-5d15-8315-e4a2fc30e721" :answer/id #uuid "949111d0-6bc2-5d15-8315-e4a2fc30e721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69523ed9-1689-5400-b80a-645cd83eb2ce" :answer/id #uuid "69523ed9-1689-5400-b80a-645cd83eb2ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9666dbcc-708b-575b-a68d-ee485b61f0dc" :answer/id #uuid "9666dbcc-708b-575b-a68d-ee485b61f0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d7d226d5-3cd0-5230-8042-7df3d54ca7fe" :activity/id #uuid "d7d226d5-3cd0-5230-8042-7df3d54ca7fe" :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048195 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-0fecd705-060a-546c-8151-ee407ac03416" "step-1b8367ed-930a-55dd-ab5d-7c8f0aba21b9" "step-27b3fa03-354c-5f28-aaed-fb655a8e3fda" "step-e5fd228f-939e-5b4e-aaa3-1ab90d20af66" "step-d8ee00f4-9dbc-522e-ba9a-dfdc9946b7a3"] :activity/first-step "step-0fecd705-060a-546c-8151-ee407ac03416" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0fecd705-060a-546c-8151-ee407ac03416" :step/id #uuid "0fecd705-060a-546c-8151-ee407ac03416" :step/content 17592186323890 :step/next "step-1b8367ed-930a-55dd-ab5d-7c8f0aba21b9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1b8367ed-930a-55dd-ab5d-7c8f0aba21b9" :step/id #uuid "1b8367ed-930a-55dd-ab5d-7c8f0aba21b9" :step/content 17592186125102 :step/next "step-27b3fa03-354c-5f28-aaed-fb655a8e3fda" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-27b3fa03-354c-5f28-aaed-fb655a8e3fda" :step/id #uuid "27b3fa03-354c-5f28-aaed-fb655a8e3fda" :step/content 17592186323949 :step/next "step-e5fd228f-939e-5b4e-aaa3-1ab90d20af66" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e5fd228f-939e-5b4e-aaa3-1ab90d20af66" :step/id #uuid "e5fd228f-939e-5b4e-aaa3-1ab90d20af66" :step/content 17592186402575 :step/next "step-d8ee00f4-9dbc-522e-ba9a-dfdc9946b7a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d8ee00f4-9dbc-522e-ba9a-dfdc9946b7a3" :step/id #uuid "d8ee00f4-9dbc-522e-ba9a-dfdc9946b7a3" :step/content "question-7f2c9542-25be-572a-a669-8d12401722e1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172309 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186387803 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262736 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" :question/id #uuid "a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" :question/math-academy-id "q-127318" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\,y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f0f5910-9dd0-5dfd-8b84-640bae613ff2" "answer-b3ac67f8-eb09-591a-819e-dd06587385a5" "answer-d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" "answer-35a88b30-d359-5319-ad67-03c9ec1379b6" "answer-3c3727fe-7ec5-539d-8680-0d97034aab9f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f0f5910-9dd0-5dfd-8b84-640bae613ff2" :answer/id #uuid "5f0f5910-9dd0-5dfd-8b84-640bae613ff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3ac67f8-eb09-591a-819e-dd06587385a5" :answer/id #uuid "b3ac67f8-eb09-591a-819e-dd06587385a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer/id #uuid "d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35a88b30-d359-5319-ad67-03c9ec1379b6" :answer/id #uuid "35a88b30-d359-5319-ad67-03c9ec1379b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c3727fe-7ec5-539d-8680-0d97034aab9f" :answer/id #uuid "3c3727fe-7ec5-539d-8680-0d97034aab9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fc8f6cbb-80b9-555e-be16-0b6e2e481fd7" :activity/id #uuid "fc8f6cbb-80b9-555e-be16-0b6e2e481fd7" :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050694 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" "step-e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" "step-e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" "step-ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6"] :activity/first-step "step-1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" :step/id #uuid "1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" :step/content 17592186172309 :step/next "step-e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" :step/id #uuid "e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" :step/content 17592186387803 :step/next "step-e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" :step/id #uuid "e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" :step/content 17592186262736 :step/next "step-ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6" :step/id #uuid "ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6" :step/content "question-a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" "question-cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" :question/id #uuid "08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162396" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 2-(2t-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(8{t}^{2}-4t+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{(8(0)^{2}-4(0)+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0-0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5755c53-01de-5e40-9f46-4f16d8b88aa1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5755c53-01de-5e40-9f46-4f16d8b88aa1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5755c53-01de-5e40-9f46-4f16d8b88aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49588260-15d3-5737-a45c-19014de13a38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1aebd026-ca42-5f7b-a004-54acb6bb5bca" "answer-49588260-15d3-5737-a45c-19014de13a38" "answer-be7a929c-eeb7-5ef6-835a-ffe7851d6867" "answer-765aabea-15c0-5261-b55c-6580766856e7" "answer-e7526dc4-7140-5b54-9556-75659724e253"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aebd026-ca42-5f7b-a004-54acb6bb5bca" :answer/id #uuid "1aebd026-ca42-5f7b-a004-54acb6bb5bca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49588260-15d3-5737-a45c-19014de13a38" :answer/id #uuid "49588260-15d3-5737-a45c-19014de13a38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be7a929c-eeb7-5ef6-835a-ffe7851d6867" :answer/id #uuid "be7a929c-eeb7-5ef6-835a-ffe7851d6867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-765aabea-15c0-5261-b55c-6580766856e7" :answer/id #uuid "765aabea-15c0-5261-b55c-6580766856e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7526dc4-7140-5b54-9556-75659724e253" :answer/id #uuid "e7526dc4-7140-5b54-9556-75659724e253" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141" :question/id #uuid "cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141" :question/math-academy-id "q-162426" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+{t}^{2})^{2},\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4t(1+{t}^{2}),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =12{t}^{2}+4,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|4t(1+{t}^{2})\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (12{t}^{2}+4)|}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}+4}{{((4t(1+{t}^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{12(0)^{2}+4}{{((4(0)(1+(0)^{2}))^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-21292018-ea56-5ae6-a7f5-dac08271bdff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21292018-ea56-5ae6-a7f5-dac08271bdff" :answer-field/id #uuid "21292018-ea56-5ae6-a7f5-dac08271bdff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a9439103-3642-5537-a910-2dc11e3a90cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef4f4a60-5e88-55c6-a337-0cc6ded7df2c" "answer-aac9c95c-bf17-5a76-803e-802d8b34fb5d" "answer-f37bf78f-c249-5ffb-8bbc-e56ecf9ea0dc" "answer-a9439103-3642-5537-a910-2dc11e3a90cd" "answer-5e0f5b62-beae-5da9-9733-3f30198a3fe8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef4f4a60-5e88-55c6-a337-0cc6ded7df2c" :answer/id #uuid "ef4f4a60-5e88-55c6-a337-0cc6ded7df2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aac9c95c-bf17-5a76-803e-802d8b34fb5d" :answer/id #uuid "aac9c95c-bf17-5a76-803e-802d8b34fb5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f37bf78f-c249-5ffb-8bbc-e56ecf9ea0dc" :answer/id #uuid "f37bf78f-c249-5ffb-8bbc-e56ecf9ea0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9439103-3642-5537-a910-2dc11e3a90cd" :answer/id #uuid "a9439103-3642-5537-a910-2dc11e3a90cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e0f5b62-beae-5da9-9733-3f30198a3fe8" :answer/id #uuid "5e0f5b62-beae-5da9-9733-3f30198a3fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b23cf47b-075d-5a97-9891-e94b2303d1ed" :activity/id #uuid "b23cf47b-075d-5a97-9891-e94b2303d1ed" :activity/title "The Curvature of a Plane Curve" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048151 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7989c10f-301e-5fd5-a99d-843967d148ab" "step-c27dc5ec-712e-5a98-ada6-dc30058e6fc4" "step-b2354326-e32e-55ec-8498-3dfd11986f23" "step-7d616550-73e3-5af7-9de1-ca6f69c3484f" "step-5984cdf7-b8da-5f68-9d43-060824a70ec7"] :activity/first-step "step-7989c10f-301e-5fd5-a99d-843967d148ab" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7989c10f-301e-5fd5-a99d-843967d148ab" :step/id #uuid "7989c10f-301e-5fd5-a99d-843967d148ab" :step/content 17592186120377 :step/next "step-c27dc5ec-712e-5a98-ada6-dc30058e6fc4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c27dc5ec-712e-5a98-ada6-dc30058e6fc4" :step/id #uuid "c27dc5ec-712e-5a98-ada6-dc30058e6fc4" :step/content "question-08715ccd-f649-5c84-af70-2145bc128aa0" :step/next "step-b2354326-e32e-55ec-8498-3dfd11986f23" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b2354326-e32e-55ec-8498-3dfd11986f23" :step/id #uuid "b2354326-e32e-55ec-8498-3dfd11986f23" :step/content 17592186281473 :step/next "step-7d616550-73e3-5af7-9de1-ca6f69c3484f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7d616550-73e3-5af7-9de1-ca6f69c3484f" :step/id #uuid "7d616550-73e3-5af7-9de1-ca6f69c3484f" :step/content "question-cf02b7af-f8d0-5528-8cc7-2d359506a141" :step/next "step-5984cdf7-b8da-5f68-9d43-060824a70ec7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5984cdf7-b8da-5f68-9d43-060824a70ec7" :step/id #uuid "5984cdf7-b8da-5f68-9d43-060824a70ec7" :step/content 17592186281490 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a" :question/id #uuid "a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163171" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{3}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{2y}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}60{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[30{e}^{2y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =120-30 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-362d2433-4c99-52b8-885b-22db14bc5af2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-362d2433-4c99-52b8-885b-22db14bc5af2" :answer-field/id #uuid "362d2433-4c99-52b8-885b-22db14bc5af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d69e97d-0fc0-5bb2-b5a3-efd04876068d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a9485ad-0d2d-50b4-b908-d8715c6808c9" "answer-54795f54-0a40-564c-911b-ae120e159861" "answer-746e5210-a7d2-5781-83fe-86be72c56d8d" "answer-6d69e97d-0fc0-5bb2-b5a3-efd04876068d" "answer-1b253e13-148e-5e5e-a20a-b5b94224af45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a9485ad-0d2d-50b4-b908-d8715c6808c9" :answer/id #uuid "1a9485ad-0d2d-50b4-b908-d8715c6808c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54795f54-0a40-564c-911b-ae120e159861" :answer/id #uuid "54795f54-0a40-564c-911b-ae120e159861" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "82\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-746e5210-a7d2-5781-83fe-86be72c56d8d" :answer/id #uuid "746e5210-a7d2-5781-83fe-86be72c56d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "86.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d69e97d-0fc0-5bb2-b5a3-efd04876068d" :answer/id #uuid "6d69e97d-0fc0-5bb2-b5a3-efd04876068d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b253e13-148e-5e5e-a20a-b5b94224af45" :answer/id #uuid "1b253e13-148e-5e5e-a20a-b5b94224af45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "94.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281622 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd5ee021dd73317a775c0ea653212f7ee5545f8c6854e6a9daabdc93b5c3b9c0.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281636 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47c897bd58a23965d9c36f683553c0817afa8bc2e37ed53a432edcd62b386e55.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}(4-y-\\frac{1}{3}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(36-27) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281637 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c2cec5e919c25623396b5ba5f052481623a868c7520aa8e3d1d3eb34f3a4a58.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(3-x-\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(8-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281644 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt[5]{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}\\cdot (-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b9cbbc27-45ef-5880-94d7-dce54c0b25ad" :activity/id #uuid "b9cbbc27-45ef-5880-94d7-dce54c0b25ad" :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049331 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" "step-9a3dcded-2a78-5806-a252-b52ade3817a0" "step-63d1ac8f-1120-57bf-a3f2-068394de0e68" "step-de27f7da-aa86-5902-bdef-f289c3a397f0" "step-6cb00066-5889-5430-a707-e706993fe08c"] :activity/first-step "step-24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" :step/id #uuid "24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" :step/content "question-a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a" :step/next "step-9a3dcded-2a78-5806-a252-b52ade3817a0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a3dcded-2a78-5806-a252-b52ade3817a0" :step/id #uuid "9a3dcded-2a78-5806-a252-b52ade3817a0" :step/content 17592186281622 :step/next "step-63d1ac8f-1120-57bf-a3f2-068394de0e68" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-63d1ac8f-1120-57bf-a3f2-068394de0e68" :step/id #uuid "63d1ac8f-1120-57bf-a3f2-068394de0e68" :step/content 17592186281636 :step/next "step-de27f7da-aa86-5902-bdef-f289c3a397f0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de27f7da-aa86-5902-bdef-f289c3a397f0" :step/id #uuid "de27f7da-aa86-5902-bdef-f289c3a397f0" :step/content 17592186281637 :step/next "step-6cb00066-5889-5430-a707-e706993fe08c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6cb00066-5889-5430-a707-e706993fe08c" :step/id #uuid "6cb00066-5889-5430-a707-e706993fe08c" :step/content 17592186281644 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129308 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bfd0e220-31b0-52ce-8e2c-55afd941a790" "question-6932ab11-a677-5ac7-a52f-b39238ad7b0e" "question-9362880e-cb69-5b9a-a326-3f6c64c489b9" "question-00632209-b439-5ff0-a973-a59862d161d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128870 :question/problem "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+(9-5)=10÷{5}^{0}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $5^0=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10÷{5}^{0}+4 & = \\\\ 10÷1+4 & = \\\\ 10+4 & = \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129309 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-372dbe69-5a97-5673-bcbc-f7d304887030" "question-6433a3cf-883a-54aa-9f03-bdc753346e58"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128871 :question/problem "What is the value of  $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129310 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" "question-5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128872 :question/problem "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n\\left(-4\\right)^{-2}  = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{(-4)}^{-2}÷{(1.5+\\frac{2}{15})}^{0} & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16}÷1 & = \\\\ 2+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{32}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{33}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bfd0e220-31b0-52ce-8e2c-55afd941a790" :question/id #uuid "bfd0e220-31b0-52ce-8e2c-55afd941a790" :question/math-academy-id "q-175947" :question/problem "$15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+(-3)^{3}÷{7}^{0} & = \\\\ 15+(-27)÷1 & = \\\\ 15+(-27) & = \\\\ 15-27 & = \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-392e6cec-195a-576a-925d-034fd1114be2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-392e6cec-195a-576a-925d-034fd1114be2" :answer-field/id #uuid "392e6cec-195a-576a-925d-034fd1114be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98" :answer/id #uuid "213a2f49-0016-5875-84c9-889f8190ab98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6932ab11-a677-5ac7-a52f-b39238ad7b0e" :question/id #uuid "6932ab11-a677-5ac7-a52f-b39238ad7b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100221" :question/problem "${(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${0.03}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 & = \\\\ {6}^{2}÷3-3 & = \\\\ 36÷3-3 & = \\\\ 12-3 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fedc1fab-9a1c-5cb6-864b-93ad4589f535"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fedc1fab-9a1c-5cb6-864b-93ad4589f535" :answer-field/id #uuid "fedc1fab-9a1c-5cb6-864b-93ad4589f535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bab4a86-358b-505d-abc3-83284ddcbca0" "answer-fee2a83e-1972-5806-bfaf-ca4442d6dce5" "answer-29cfea49-9f6f-5f9a-bae2-ad1521302d21" "answer-0a5b659f-28dd-59a3-9a57-f1486c9e4227" "answer-37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bab4a86-358b-505d-abc3-83284ddcbca0" :answer/id #uuid "7bab4a86-358b-505d-abc3-83284ddcbca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee2a83e-1972-5806-bfaf-ca4442d6dce5" :answer/id #uuid "fee2a83e-1972-5806-bfaf-ca4442d6dce5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29cfea49-9f6f-5f9a-bae2-ad1521302d21" :answer/id #uuid "29cfea49-9f6f-5f9a-bae2-ad1521302d21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a5b659f-28dd-59a3-9a57-f1486c9e4227" :answer/id #uuid "0a5b659f-28dd-59a3-9a57-f1486c9e4227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "78" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6" :answer/id #uuid "37d0909f-7fbe-5218-bffa-a0ad06486fc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9362880e-cb69-5b9a-a326-3f6c64c489b9" :question/id #uuid "9362880e-cb69-5b9a-a326-3f6c64c489b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-178596" :question/problem "$((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nNotice that $(-4)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((-4)^{0}-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (1-5)÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ -4÷4+7 & = \\\\ -1+7 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-609c007e-c457-5fc9-a429-2f89995ec208"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-609c007e-c457-5fc9-a429-2f89995ec208" :answer-field/id #uuid "609c007e-c457-5fc9-a429-2f89995ec208" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2" :answer/id #uuid "417d82f2-955e-5809-8bcc-e9b8832280b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-00632209-b439-5ff0-a973-a59862d161d8" :question/id #uuid "00632209-b439-5ff0-a973-a59862d161d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-178582" :question/problem "${8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${8}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{0}+{2}^{3}\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+8\\times 4 & = \\\\ 1+(8\\times 4) & = \\\\ 1+32 & = \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b2d042f-d653-58ff-8c3e-46fccccb3798"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b2d042f-d653-58ff-8c3e-46fccccb3798" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2d042f-d653-58ff-8c3e-46fccccb3798" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf" :answer/id #uuid "6e30d5f8-b029-5e8f-994f-f9d19469b0bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-372dbe69-5a97-5673-bcbc-f7d304887030" :question/id #uuid "372dbe69-5a97-5673-bcbc-f7d304887030" :question/math-academy-id "q-100293" :question/problem "${7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6+1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 7}{7\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{7}}{3\\times \\cancel{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aed2c6d7-4afd-5487-8b46-d9d9acb8c258"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aed2c6d7-4afd-5487-8b46-d9d9acb8c258" :answer-field/id #uuid "aed2c6d7-4afd-5487-8b46-d9d9acb8c258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" "answer-031fe889-8bcf-55b6-9e2c-5bc76ff27045" "answer-2e9c3feb-96eb-537a-af4e-a8b745ab09c2" "answer-f9d07389-9d7e-558b-836a-ab159ef21592" "answer-18283892-3eb2-55cd-b35f-59a9010c6395"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" :answer/id #uuid "43f5d7d0-17b2-565d-a98f-1e82335af6f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-031fe889-8bcf-55b6-9e2c-5bc76ff27045" :answer/id #uuid "031fe889-8bcf-55b6-9e2c-5bc76ff27045" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e9c3feb-96eb-537a-af4e-a8b745ab09c2" :answer/id #uuid "2e9c3feb-96eb-537a-af4e-a8b745ab09c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9d07389-9d7e-558b-836a-ab159ef21592" :answer/id #uuid "f9d07389-9d7e-558b-836a-ab159ef21592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18283892-3eb2-55cd-b35f-59a9010c6395" :answer/id #uuid "18283892-3eb2-55cd-b35f-59a9010c6395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6433a3cf-883a-54aa-9f03-bdc753346e58" :question/id #uuid "6433a3cf-883a-54aa-9f03-bdc753346e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-175958" :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+(5+1)^{-2}=\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${6}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{36}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+{6}^{-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times 2+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{36}\\times \\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 2}{36\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{36}+\\frac{1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{10+1}{36} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3bea4dc1-0a50-536e-8358-41147aeb0b3f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3bea4dc1-0a50-536e-8358-41147aeb0b3f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3bea4dc1-0a50-536e-8358-41147aeb0b3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8" :answer/id #uuid "ff108b6d-b341-5dec-bcea-441b760861b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" :question/id #uuid "2dbc86c1-eb39-5436-87d8-eb16a5d4d270" :question/math-academy-id "q-179401" :question/problem "$(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{0} & = \\\\ {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that ${2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{1+16}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{16}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c9a275f8-e12f-531b-9ed6-2c3eda31cf0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9a275f8-e12f-531b-9ed6-2c3eda31cf0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a275f8-e12f-531b-9ed6-2c3eda31cf0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/id #uuid "ff37ba3f-b657-5468-a046-c7a885ae86ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf" :question/id #uuid "5e5e796d-b724-597d-a614-e8af03095fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-100301" :question/problem "Calculate ${(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}={(\\frac{2}{1})}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3} & = \\\\ 1-11\\times 8 & = \\\\ 1-88 & = \\\\ -87\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93d546fe-e93e-5d3c-8f24-4f640019211e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93d546fe-e93e-5d3c-8f24-4f640019211e" :answer-field/id #uuid "93d546fe-e93e-5d3c-8f24-4f640019211e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdeff659-436b-5f34-ac81-a6409e40fce1" "answer-2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" "answer-b381b4b1-c1bc-52ab-8b7e-21ef5860d874" "answer-76d69649-f0c6-5771-848d-71954873581b" "answer-e6602306-1f53-57b5-91c7-8b39e35659a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdeff659-436b-5f34-ac81-a6409e40fce1" :answer/id #uuid "fdeff659-436b-5f34-ac81-a6409e40fce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-121" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/id #uuid "2f5e6d20-6d83-5a4b-9919-511354ddc37f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b381b4b1-c1bc-52ab-8b7e-21ef5860d874" :answer/id #uuid "b381b4b1-c1bc-52ab-8b7e-21ef5860d874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d69649-f0c6-5771-848d-71954873581b" :answer/id #uuid "76d69649-f0c6-5771-848d-71954873581b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6602306-1f53-57b5-91c7-8b39e35659a8" :answer/id #uuid "e6602306-1f53-57b5-91c7-8b39e35659a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130481 :tutorial/content "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{0}+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+4÷2 & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129335 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-75f0d82a-dd66-5d66-8858-8e85c7e74ab7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-75f0d82a-dd66-5d66-8858-8e85c7e74ab7" :question/id #uuid "75f0d82a-dd66-5d66-8858-8e85c7e74ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94610" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{a{t}^{3}+bt+1\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)=t+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =(t+1)+(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)=t+1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2(t+1)=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a24ecf47-9c4a-5174-a968-bb79cd6b4d3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a24ecf47-9c4a-5174-a968-bb79cd6b4d3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "a24ecf47-9c4a-5174-a968-bb79cd6b4d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6d54a74-6c6a-5da4-9499-7d05f911b9ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f0c6a78-5937-5a8c-b3a3-be973c02f31d" "answer-62c9945e-be6a-5851-bfe4-b314ac1b71d1" "answer-0be6e3fd-a982-5d87-84bd-f85e41333d20" "answer-06c159e3-322b-5045-961d-a4bb6de6bf28" "answer-a6d54a74-6c6a-5da4-9499-7d05f911b9ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f0c6a78-5937-5a8c-b3a3-be973c02f31d" :answer/id #uuid "7f0c6a78-5937-5a8c-b3a3-be973c02f31d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62c9945e-be6a-5851-bfe4-b314ac1b71d1" :answer/id #uuid "62c9945e-be6a-5851-bfe4-b314ac1b71d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0be6e3fd-a982-5d87-84bd-f85e41333d20" :answer/id #uuid "0be6e3fd-a982-5d87-84bd-f85e41333d20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06c159e3-322b-5045-961d-a4bb6de6bf28" :answer/id #uuid "06c159e3-322b-5045-961d-a4bb6de6bf28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6d54a74-6c6a-5da4-9499-7d05f911b9ab" :answer/id #uuid "a6d54a74-6c6a-5da4-9499-7d05f911b9ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-41cad912-e122-5581-bcb3-a15aeb801b3b" "question-17e0153f-17ba-57ee-a920-3117ebdc5b12" "question-4b9728bb-13e5-5813-917f-2ed0c37ac7bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133602 :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](images/23/23aedd2796ca55056409a6d0c32e0d5521283297398bd78d8c7ac3ce84c42fba.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/10/10e08ad8dd61b256018aa33488f94b563669fa91c739785488306d15160838c9.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/6e/6ec9d05c93c9ae7a3c3079d69b7c0b5d665fb295efaa918cea26f77988110342.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a9/a9fa0f03fc16c2d3f1c2102033c0ec6c7d4911ee2e1001a460893c85171204b8.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133603 :question/problem "Consider the subspace $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $\\mathcal B$ are given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}?$\n\n![](images/b2/b20aeaf492050b26176c993d64442f214c39d24092989ec1c75b5e2ca9673947.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n![](images/fa/fa7bd1a6125c6796bd5689409a22c34081e78027dbaff1356efecddfe755b323.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/0f/0f65ef9e505d3e961d0330c1ff6d52d5239eff91811a61b4878d6a2f13923bff.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/be/be2842c481ecd55360f9bc3054e2535d4a92bbb8ebd77f169522c329ff2d715d.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ed119e3d-d405-5ea0-ae9c-40defc91b776"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133604 :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$\n'\n![](images/3a/3ae8f3b146aea78019b441917a80b2da118044c207cbf42c4b9fe2b51c3920d2.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/f0/f04136fae84ebaf8ac272b48ae3d6fda95c26df1a4c3c1c800d261ab8acf0821.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/7e/7e7c38a548b58edae501a2f881bb9feb0553eb9ae51a9de96726233a9ce0543b.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ![](images/c2/c2e0f7b0e96c4d945bbf80d262b68366db85df52a754410d39602f4ba37fc4d4.svg)\n\n- ![](images/8c/8c16a541393a8060313140e677fc1e9347b7145c505f7ddb5bdae2111f68e216.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/06/06d515f38454768bd2ec372b917828bbb7a99ee07ea5aa4745a10e5ff1ecdfcf.svg) is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-41cad912-e122-5581-bcb3-a15aeb801b3b" :question/id #uuid "41cad912-e122-5581-bcb3-a15aeb801b3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41031" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-363499bf-09e7-5ef8-9f85-08b9df560e62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-363499bf-09e7-5ef8-9f85-08b9df560e62" :answer-field/id #uuid "363499bf-09e7-5ef8-9f85-08b9df560e62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2466579e-51e6-5724-b11f-494b5cbd91ea" "answer-0d9e8e1d-513e-59b9-9746-2585e1481503" "answer-d058a80e-4564-5675-b58b-217af9b2adce" "answer-5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" "answer-67ccaa28-3669-5421-86da-e8251e588b2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2466579e-51e6-5724-b11f-494b5cbd91ea" :answer/id #uuid "2466579e-51e6-5724-b11f-494b5cbd91ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d9e8e1d-513e-59b9-9746-2585e1481503" :answer/id #uuid "0d9e8e1d-513e-59b9-9746-2585e1481503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d058a80e-4564-5675-b58b-217af9b2adce" :answer/id #uuid "d058a80e-4564-5675-b58b-217af9b2adce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" :answer/id #uuid "5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ccaa28-3669-5421-86da-e8251e588b2b" :answer/id #uuid "67ccaa28-3669-5421-86da-e8251e588b2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17e0153f-17ba-57ee-a920-3117ebdc5b12" :question/id #uuid "17e0153f-17ba-57ee-a920-3117ebdc5b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-140524" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5e8feed-0144-5565-9376-5bcd8f09bbb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5e8feed-0144-5565-9376-5bcd8f09bbb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e8feed-0144-5565-9376-5bcd8f09bbb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fdbf6e7-3003-543d-b80a-6884375af46b" "answer-b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" "answer-91a07927-047f-58d6-9e1b-00da5c4d3b96" "answer-391ed7ae-6243-537b-a07f-f3dade005876" "answer-35fb792f-f499-55f0-beb5-0a36ea500f74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fdbf6e7-3003-543d-b80a-6884375af46b" :answer/id #uuid "8fdbf6e7-3003-543d-b80a-6884375af46b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" :answer/id #uuid "b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91a07927-047f-58d6-9e1b-00da5c4d3b96" :answer/id #uuid "91a07927-047f-58d6-9e1b-00da5c4d3b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-391ed7ae-6243-537b-a07f-f3dade005876" :answer/id #uuid "391ed7ae-6243-537b-a07f-f3dade005876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35fb792f-f499-55f0-beb5-0a36ea500f74" :answer/id #uuid "35fb792f-f499-55f0-beb5-0a36ea500f74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351261 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{13}{3})}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186414751 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b9728bb-13e5-5813-917f-2ed0c37ac7bc" :question/id #uuid "4b9728bb-13e5-5813-917f-2ed0c37ac7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86847" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=1,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1c3af90-4b4e-5817-986c-ad8b162022f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1c3af90-4b4e-5817-986c-ad8b162022f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c3af90-4b4e-5817-986c-ad8b162022f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8567ab33-79b0-5c93-ab46-2e6895393cb5" "answer-2dcb854a-8359-5e93-87cf-89bffc23af38" "answer-30e0bd06-5ba4-5322-8aaa-c8efbd6bb22c" "answer-16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" "answer-54604561-d490-5cf0-8396-166d4506693f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8567ab33-79b0-5c93-ab46-2e6895393cb5" :answer/id #uuid "8567ab33-79b0-5c93-ab46-2e6895393cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dcb854a-8359-5e93-87cf-89bffc23af38" :answer/id #uuid "2dcb854a-8359-5e93-87cf-89bffc23af38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30e0bd06-5ba4-5322-8aaa-c8efbd6bb22c" :answer/id #uuid "30e0bd06-5ba4-5322-8aaa-c8efbd6bb22c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" :answer/id #uuid "16f8819e-1e45-5b70-843f-9474e62016e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54604561-d490-5cf0-8396-166d4506693f" :answer/id #uuid "54604561-d490-5cf0-8396-166d4506693f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366379 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366125 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326873 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=\\frac{10}{3},$ $c=\\frac{5}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(\\frac{5}{3})}=2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272123 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354525 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{(-\\frac{13}{3})}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366386 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366118 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{1}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351265 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{4}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $b=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41},$ $c=\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{3\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|-\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|+|\\frac{4\\sqrt{41}}{41}|=\\frac{11\\sqrt{41}}{41}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272130 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ed119e3d-d405-5ea0-ae9c-40defc91b776" :question/id #uuid "ed119e3d-d405-5ea0-ae9c-40defc91b776" :question/math-academy-id "q-86852" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46},$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{23}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d04279b-ec75-5254-bd55-34cd8f8839e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d04279b-ec75-5254-bd55-34cd8f8839e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d04279b-ec75-5254-bd55-34cd8f8839e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a891591d-a9eb-53fe-8982-96c9b063fffc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2214cfa-1da7-5079-9e10-7f06971759ac" "answer-a891591d-a9eb-53fe-8982-96c9b063fffc" "answer-85a5f849-bfe1-54f4-a472-2f3b1a9f7def" "answer-7f1f7ef1-8ffa-5d79-89f5-0f699ae92a81" "answer-b8569b62-9dc0-5a84-afdd-7c7913cb7cbd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2214cfa-1da7-5079-9e10-7f06971759ac" :answer/id #uuid "c2214cfa-1da7-5079-9e10-7f06971759ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{46}}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a891591d-a9eb-53fe-8982-96c9b063fffc" :answer/id #uuid "a891591d-a9eb-53fe-8982-96c9b063fffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85a5f849-bfe1-54f4-a472-2f3b1a9f7def" :answer/id #uuid "85a5f849-bfe1-54f4-a472-2f3b1a9f7def" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{46}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f1f7ef1-8ffa-5d79-89f5-0f699ae92a81" :answer/id #uuid "7f1f7ef1-8ffa-5d79-89f5-0f699ae92a81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8569b62-9dc0-5a84-afdd-7c7913cb7cbd" :answer/id #uuid "b8569b62-9dc0-5a84-afdd-7c7913cb7cbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/c2/c2543117bc661286a5965534dfeeaaf6a066281ee58e2f39ea35143971d6837f.svg)\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting ![](images/72/7221215504f7dc7a831501ee23c0563434dd414ac05c1ef7e5761c2719e7d24d.svg) Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/59/5944c42ffd5e609dcec914718180be9652a817de6b8cf84439d57cb106f03353.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135089 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n![](images/79/79d1a4963f0e8a2e47dda0acf5c6031571574b1631d57c15d757f94b4f25468b.svg)\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n![](images/c6/c69d0c835aa55e7e835c81a4338d56bd3fda97ebb8a73ed370e4384b80d23093.svg)\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/fc/fcdac688252b50d74ed8c4bddba578f027f3bfec5a356ec3fe54839638c1a0ae.svg)\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134006 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119855 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" "question-379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323698 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 17 & k-12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-17){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=k+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+5,$ which means that $k=-5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" :question/id #uuid "66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" :question/math-academy-id "q-74210" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(C).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ then the equation $Cx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 0=k-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a46153c-e7f9-5a37-81cc-90b7f7320e59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a46153c-e7f9-5a37-81cc-90b7f7320e59" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a46153c-e7f9-5a37-81cc-90b7f7320e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95981581-0e25-501f-b8a4-5a0f6cf1319e" "answer-db545c16-037d-5d5e-bd83-57a3b42fa7ab" "answer-ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" "answer-d3299a9a-dd1e-5fcf-906a-3fe1e9c9e612" "answer-191150d3-af6c-5369-8a94-a6bfdd39b6f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95981581-0e25-501f-b8a4-5a0f6cf1319e" :answer/id #uuid "95981581-0e25-501f-b8a4-5a0f6cf1319e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db545c16-037d-5d5e-bd83-57a3b42fa7ab" :answer/id #uuid "db545c16-037d-5d5e-bd83-57a3b42fa7ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" :answer/id #uuid "ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3299a9a-dd1e-5fcf-906a-3fe1e9c9e612" :answer/id #uuid "d3299a9a-dd1e-5fcf-906a-3fe1e9c9e612" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-191150d3-af6c-5369-8a94-a6bfdd39b6f0" :answer/id #uuid "191150d3-af6c-5369-8a94-a6bfdd39b6f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" :question/id #uuid "379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" :question/math-academy-id "q-110978" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ k+30\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{2}=7 \\\\ 0=k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" "answer-6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" "answer-b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" "answer-2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" "answer-e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" :answer/id #uuid "c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" :answer/id #uuid "6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" :answer/id #uuid "b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/id #uuid "2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af" :answer/id #uuid "e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186408162 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  second equation, we get a false statement $0=6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4195ab3a-5a74-5c79-a379-9f09ccd21301" :activity/id #uuid "4195ab3a-5a74-5c79-a379-9f09ccd21301" :activity/title "The Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050656 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-86ec8e45-63d4-5d25-a4e3-e63dc0aaaa33" "step-18815fe1-7a23-50e9-b4e6-4a08c8787198" "step-ffca9ec5-be0b-5330-9fd1-5e439c5ad7d1" "step-8889818c-a946-5d8d-bda5-8076c4a7ea11" "step-34d92445-9658-5baa-b2e8-ecaa236d882b"] :