ages/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) be ![](images/45/45ba004dac2f7f8efb58a5071b600dded19307f6fbc2bc376cc780ec2054b4fa.svg) where\n\n![](images/bc/bccb7b1dec5f98fa287663b1c37d604bcb342be948cb205017e0a6aa9598efc8.svg)\n\nand the supports of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg), respectively, are\n\n![](images/6d/6d05be65088fd21af5b4b56f7ad17ff4068ea78f1f476384412a1de0aed78238.svg)\n\nFind ![](images/00/00697601602b2e721dd6f603e9b1fe88e2b06035d91f165719a5b9fc56c5aba9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF ![](images/7f/7f3705d5dab288c1c6774f437aa18dea961252d741fb302b399cb2ad18112a3a.svg) we can think of each point where ![](images/d1/d17e98e4e46a13b4d2d8f97bbcf008c339ed281a929aa3eec7ecdd7b63a9ac92.svg) as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF ![](images/24/246748e439e0fd3d76529b637ab1ac09d2308e45a826924cee283c1e9252e6ea.svg) is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/07/07fd689b67975f50da1b6b025460996fc87f21f758f5e98d34eb84c9b0e50fcd.svg) the joint support ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) is a subset of\n\n![](images/b9/b9de46a0daabfc623c17c8366760c1ad801f11931fa9e274a660a25ce19977aa.svg)\n\nLet's plot the points in ![](images/48/48162c9ef32c06765d5fc93f0f8d7e71457d6951bea5d902700551014a70650f.svg) which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/91/91a0796929c7d023dd3a7110458ec2b9b3c365b8ba433c001707155c65cf4e28.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at ![](images/6b/6b867b7633ee52cd50377faf42b2bf05d25b7b7985fb452484e77c842c4869b4.svg) and top-left corner at ![](images/f9/f9312dff657ae3bae42b12f4862babdc044a9557a23a0afbd40c8efeafbe2734.svg) where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/80/80d02d152f41e88c9533d06887a925a6acd9438924b36a3c1268e04d150ea1ac.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/6b/6b867b7633ee52cd50377faf42b2bf05d25b7b7985fb452484e77c842c4869b4.svg) (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/0a/0aef6691a5c584a00fb09bbbfa667f205f5819bc0ff10de5f8e53b207de0a70c.svg) (the red region below).\n\n![](images/d0/d094730505c58c936e5d4313ca9a9fc9c6ff9fd117f591a9382ef0719f8b8df3.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/6d/6d92c2320b23f59414b763322db5af452fe7b1209c2983410e0e8959f1d1e38b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-40beaebe-e39b-5263-8664-2ca0bd457e93" :question/id #uuid "40beaebe-e39b-5263-8664-2ca0bd457e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-146338" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 0.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 0}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,0)+f(2,0) \\\\  & =0.35+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04e92015-0288-5022-836f-fa11f4607dd5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04e92015-0288-5022-836f-fa11f4607dd5" :answer-field/id #uuid "04e92015-0288-5022-836f-fa11f4607dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63bbbc94-2421-5b0d-8245-dc342d764984" "answer-2bbc5089-2da3-5298-974d-ece99d9afcd6" "answer-dc2b7f5a-d6cd-5bd8-9453-67d08d568d72" "answer-5bb4c7f7-e3ac-5e28-8130-565a87c9fd9e" "answer-4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63bbbc94-2421-5b0d-8245-dc342d764984" :answer/id #uuid "63bbbc94-2421-5b0d-8245-dc342d764984" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bbc5089-2da3-5298-974d-ece99d9afcd6" :answer/id #uuid "2bbc5089-2da3-5298-974d-ece99d9afcd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc2b7f5a-d6cd-5bd8-9453-67d08d568d72" :answer/id #uuid "dc2b7f5a-d6cd-5bd8-9453-67d08d568d72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bb4c7f7-e3ac-5e28-8130-565a87c9fd9e" :answer/id #uuid "5bb4c7f7-e3ac-5e28-8130-565a87c9fd9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" :answer/id #uuid "4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf85ed85-426f-57b2-8e6b-2867b23718fe" :question/id #uuid "bf85ed85-426f-57b2-8e6b-2867b23718fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-146340" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 1,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 1}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,2)+f(-1,4)+f(1,2)+f(1,4) \\\\  & =0.3+0.2+0.2+0.1 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ecb7351-8bb5-5d22-b044-3cded2d7c3e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ecb7351-8bb5-5d22-b044-3cded2d7c3e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ecb7351-8bb5-5d22-b044-3cded2d7c3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a81cd295-d38e-53da-85d2-1b6969704637" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16af02e7-285b-536f-be29-deb33861f730" "answer-38a04b40-4ef4-5360-a44b-8dc3b1b4e263" "answer-1775b091-f477-561c-bdab-40f3bfe4397b" "answer-7cb8bf91-cf4d-5248-b68a-d4485bd18fa0" "answer-a81cd295-d38e-53da-85d2-1b6969704637"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16af02e7-285b-536f-be29-deb33861f730" :answer/id #uuid "16af02e7-285b-536f-be29-deb33861f730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38a04b40-4ef4-5360-a44b-8dc3b1b4e263" :answer/id #uuid "38a04b40-4ef4-5360-a44b-8dc3b1b4e263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1775b091-f477-561c-bdab-40f3bfe4397b" :answer/id #uuid "1775b091-f477-561c-bdab-40f3bfe4397b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cb8bf91-cf4d-5248-b68a-d4485bd18fa0" :answer/id #uuid "7cb8bf91-cf4d-5248-b68a-d4485bd18fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a81cd295-d38e-53da-85d2-1b6969704637" :answer/id #uuid "a81cd295-d38e-53da-85d2-1b6969704637" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c34fa369-1de0-5740-997b-e34e018ecb92" :question/id #uuid "c34fa369-1de0-5740-997b-e34e018ecb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-146389" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) \\\\  & =0.2+0.3+0.1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-960a006f-092b-5cc0-ae1b-106bba927b26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-960a006f-092b-5cc0-ae1b-106bba927b26" :answer-field/id #uuid "960a006f-092b-5cc0-ae1b-106bba927b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b16c880f-0f86-582b-ae1f-223c2802503d" "answer-59fa7bbf-b27b-58e4-a896-8340b8f2bdb7" "answer-5cff4ee6-31ec-5c09-907b-5027d5a24e87" "answer-7ab89f8e-8046-55ef-a939-0ea4407202fe" "answer-1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b16c880f-0f86-582b-ae1f-223c2802503d" :answer/id #uuid "b16c880f-0f86-582b-ae1f-223c2802503d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59fa7bbf-b27b-58e4-a896-8340b8f2bdb7" :answer/id #uuid "59fa7bbf-b27b-58e4-a896-8340b8f2bdb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cff4ee6-31ec-5c09-907b-5027d5a24e87" :answer/id #uuid "5cff4ee6-31ec-5c09-907b-5027d5a24e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ab89f8e-8046-55ef-a939-0ea4407202fe" :answer/id #uuid "7ab89f8e-8046-55ef-a939-0ea4407202fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" :answer/id #uuid "1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1b40ecf-b8ac-57c2-a3f6-1fe3744073eb" :question/id #uuid "a1b40ecf-b8ac-57c2-a3f6-1fe3744073eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-146339" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3,-1) & =P(X\\le 3,Y\\le -1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 3,Y\\le -1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3,-1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 3}{\\sum }_{y\\le -1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+f(2,-1)+f(3,-1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05+0.05 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-988f25cc-e89c-5973-86d6-a8e7cf03401d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-988f25cc-e89c-5973-86d6-a8e7cf03401d" :answer-field/id #uuid "988f25cc-e89c-5973-86d6-a8e7cf03401d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34a329eb-5de4-5c2c-91b5-bbf771286324" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f23e9a7-1aff-5593-8cd8-bf00cca95e61" "answer-cf71fb52-a5c4-5c92-98f7-9007ecf29544" "answer-34a329eb-5de4-5c2c-91b5-bbf771286324" "answer-2eab1c44-22c1-5922-aa81-6d6e0b8b3b7f" "answer-7f367159-bd7a-56d4-be64-6bad11e5245d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f23e9a7-1aff-5593-8cd8-bf00cca95e61" :answer/id #uuid "7f23e9a7-1aff-5593-8cd8-bf00cca95e61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf71fb52-a5c4-5c92-98f7-9007ecf29544" :answer/id #uuid "cf71fb52-a5c4-5c92-98f7-9007ecf29544" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34a329eb-5de4-5c2c-91b5-bbf771286324" :answer/id #uuid "34a329eb-5de4-5c2c-91b5-bbf771286324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eab1c44-22c1-5922-aa81-6d6e0b8b3b7f" :answer/id #uuid "2eab1c44-22c1-5922-aa81-6d6e0b8b3b7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f367159-bd7a-56d4-be64-6bad11e5245d" :answer/id #uuid "7f367159-bd7a-56d4-be64-6bad11e5245d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162411 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,4)+f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159668 :answer-field/correct 17592186159672 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9db88b4b-2611-5ba3-82d8-88e768790963" :question/id #uuid "9db88b4b-2611-5ba3-82d8-88e768790963" :question/math-academy-id "q-146412" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3,y<1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3$ and $y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/2e/2ef3347831324b55e1a8ddaff2b0eb6af7fb7fc171867906e70c4b51f0051b05.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<1,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bff05c4f-8ecf-52a9-80bb-3008a1ac8466"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bff05c4f-8ecf-52a9-80bb-3008a1ac8466" :answer-field/id #uuid "bff05c4f-8ecf-52a9-80bb-3008a1ac8466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15f9f3f5-8540-5419-9152-05a79fde0503" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15f9f3f5-8540-5419-9152-05a79fde0503" "answer-467b4c1a-52dd-5e7b-a4b8-c526edfc555c" "answer-4283f191-e6ae-59e2-8352-5a32b8759e3e" "answer-a16f48d3-229c-5a5b-aeaf-31b628730ee4" "answer-7b0415f8-e7ba-54a2-884f-2cd57c2a8884"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15f9f3f5-8540-5419-9152-05a79fde0503" :answer/id #uuid "15f9f3f5-8540-5419-9152-05a79fde0503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-467b4c1a-52dd-5e7b-a4b8-c526edfc555c" :answer/id #uuid "467b4c1a-52dd-5e7b-a4b8-c526edfc555c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4283f191-e6ae-59e2-8352-5a32b8759e3e" :answer/id #uuid "4283f191-e6ae-59e2-8352-5a32b8759e3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a16f48d3-229c-5a5b-aeaf-31b628730ee4" :answer/id #uuid "a16f48d3-229c-5a5b-aeaf-31b628730ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b0415f8-e7ba-54a2-884f-2cd57c2a8884" :answer/id #uuid "7b0415f8-e7ba-54a2-884f-2cd57c2a8884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f664747-7f72-51aa-bfa2-d2cff4da1dfc" :question/id #uuid "5f664747-7f72-51aa-bfa2-d2cff4da1dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-146416" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,2<y<3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2\\}\\times \\{2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0$ and $2<y<3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/64dbc5d811486be64e3dd87b151d9ae8f2ae4b2cf36c3964d32d0439843a2bb5.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at least $0$ and $x<0,$ no possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7622902-72a2-5f8e-afeb-0b6e3959d439"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7622902-72a2-5f8e-afeb-0b6e3959d439" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7622902-72a2-5f8e-afeb-0b6e3959d439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46f5ca98-7d78-5832-a1d0-06cf550eedc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46f5ca98-7d78-5832-a1d0-06cf550eedc9" "answer-ea9877a1-2066-5bfe-a93c-622a26463892" "answer-cdb85c49-b87b-5100-b881-117c6132a661" "answer-fef50f4b-c336-5d8a-8f57-78204f060675" "answer-10f89b01-601b-5b2c-ae0c-644dbbe8e6b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46f5ca98-7d78-5832-a1d0-06cf550eedc9" :answer/id #uuid "46f5ca98-7d78-5832-a1d0-06cf550eedc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea9877a1-2066-5bfe-a93c-622a26463892" :answer/id #uuid "ea9877a1-2066-5bfe-a93c-622a26463892" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdb85c49-b87b-5100-b881-117c6132a661" :answer/id #uuid "cdb85c49-b87b-5100-b881-117c6132a661" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef50f4b-c336-5d8a-8f57-78204f060675" :answer/id #uuid "fef50f4b-c336-5d8a-8f57-78204f060675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10f89b01-601b-5b2c-ae0c-644dbbe8e6b3" :answer/id #uuid "10f89b01-601b-5b2c-ae0c-644dbbe8e6b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5d1cc22-e243-59a9-86db-8bc6845a4180" :question/id #uuid "c5d1cc22-e243-59a9-86db-8bc6845a4180" :question/math-academy-id "q-146414" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>4,y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,4\\}\\times \\{0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>4$ and $y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ef/efa1ab8112333d8b91715568f5ed08c26734a6b55b1fe0a8f692beae3b92a52f.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $0$ and $y<0,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0cf8db09-45a8-58a8-91d6-44d151fe91d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0cf8db09-45a8-58a8-91d6-44d151fe91d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf8db09-45a8-58a8-91d6-44d151fe91d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06b7327c-aa3d-54f5-8903-ac2fa2aea07a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06b7327c-aa3d-54f5-8903-ac2fa2aea07a" "answer-d9d15474-28c5-52fd-b92c-fd09019100b6" "answer-212d92a1-3461-5e5f-9eb6-0c82d582f22e" "answer-26a42208-487a-5e71-980b-447e3606bb4c" "answer-4a2a74ed-d118-5d4e-8070-d92b2f3a20af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06b7327c-aa3d-54f5-8903-ac2fa2aea07a" :answer/id #uuid "06b7327c-aa3d-54f5-8903-ac2fa2aea07a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9d15474-28c5-52fd-b92c-fd09019100b6" :answer/id #uuid "d9d15474-28c5-52fd-b92c-fd09019100b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-212d92a1-3461-5e5f-9eb6-0c82d582f22e" :answer/id #uuid "212d92a1-3461-5e5f-9eb6-0c82d582f22e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26a42208-487a-5e71-980b-447e3606bb4c" :answer/id #uuid "26a42208-487a-5e71-980b-447e3606bb4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2a74ed-d118-5d4e-8070-d92b2f3a20af" :answer/id #uuid "4a2a74ed-d118-5d4e-8070-d92b2f3a20af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5555465c-a779-5eab-9f3a-3128b4e5cfe0" :question/id #uuid "5555465c-a779-5eab-9f3a-3128b4e5cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146409" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,y>6.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>6,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/644db930cdb0d683a0bcae61b2596dbaeaa4c46df45d8347053c742d2ded3af9.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $1$ and $x>1,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $6$ and $y>6,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c47eb18a-bfe5-5e49-bb9e-a97fe694f936"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c47eb18a-bfe5-5e49-bb9e-a97fe694f936" :answer-field/id #uuid "c47eb18a-bfe5-5e49-bb9e-a97fe694f936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc7c9232-17f6-5028-afcd-f94db070b33d" "answer-616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" "answer-d2c3a3c7-6f3b-5337-8e2c-82b7f32fd2ed" "answer-b3aa2cdd-73fe-5eb0-a310-26b21184ef73" "answer-f5c1b40c-90e0-5d0c-8b12-94ea0606a002"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc7c9232-17f6-5028-afcd-f94db070b33d" :answer/id #uuid "fc7c9232-17f6-5028-afcd-f94db070b33d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" :answer/id #uuid "616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c3a3c7-6f3b-5337-8e2c-82b7f32fd2ed" :answer/id #uuid "d2c3a3c7-6f3b-5337-8e2c-82b7f32fd2ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3aa2cdd-73fe-5eb0-a310-26b21184ef73" :answer/id #uuid "b3aa2cdd-73fe-5eb0-a310-26b21184ef73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5c1b40c-90e0-5d0c-8b12-94ea0606a002" :answer/id #uuid "f5c1b40c-90e0-5d0c-8b12-94ea0606a002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65aabd4c-4895-5cab-b402-d575edfca45d" :question/id #uuid "65aabd4c-4895-5cab-b402-d575edfca45d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146413" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $2<x<4,y<2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,3,4\\}\\times \\{2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $2<x<4$ and $y<2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4c/4cfff97ef922ffe99f880771d525d616e6c5b601406597813d63425e5ab19345.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $2$ and $y<2,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6579d250-258d-5c0c-bab1-24d9d82f47f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6579d250-258d-5c0c-bab1-24d9d82f47f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "6579d250-258d-5c0c-bab1-24d9d82f47f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-656297a8-093a-51d0-b0b2-bf686ace95ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-656297a8-093a-51d0-b0b2-bf686ace95ce" "answer-ac05513f-e7d8-53b9-b6e5-c2eb2777b062" "answer-c19ad8b6-03e1-5d59-bffb-90119dcabc97" "answer-da24238e-0493-50a7-845e-72c9199d84d9" "answer-7f25d856-0b5c-51b6-92ea-4f8d7b41b8db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-656297a8-093a-51d0-b0b2-bf686ace95ce" :answer/id #uuid "656297a8-093a-51d0-b0b2-bf686ace95ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac05513f-e7d8-53b9-b6e5-c2eb2777b062" :answer/id #uuid "ac05513f-e7d8-53b9-b6e5-c2eb2777b062" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c19ad8b6-03e1-5d59-bffb-90119dcabc97" :answer/id #uuid "c19ad8b6-03e1-5d59-bffb-90119dcabc97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da24238e-0493-50a7-845e-72c9199d84d9" :answer/id #uuid "da24238e-0493-50a7-845e-72c9199d84d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f25d856-0b5c-51b6-92ea-4f8d7b41b8db" :answer/id #uuid "7f25d856-0b5c-51b6-92ea-4f8d7b41b8db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af981bc5-9b24-55d4-b993-5b515801ef39" :question/id #uuid "af981bc5-9b24-55d4-b993-5b515801ef39" :question/math-academy-id "q-146626" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,1)=0.6,\\,F(1,0)=0.5,\\,F(1,-1)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,-1<Y\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/dd/dd49cc4b7cb5cc80bb325819c5c8f3c31f8bc3ed762139c2c3b9567a24db2324.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/34/344fec686907843b4b9375db138592f93be9d0232500fb35dceb90086db503cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e3a7389008d67f4096fdaa6a98c0e3e2bdeda64ec1e47b93a6d3ba66a54b18d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f967f58-22eb-508f-8d10-16ff3515e346"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f967f58-22eb-508f-8d10-16ff3515e346" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f967f58-22eb-508f-8d10-16ff3515e346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a550f44-e8e6-5b84-9bae-f67384a3896a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-031a5be1-80e3-55f8-96a4-84489190a8dc" "answer-67b84b0f-4bfb-52d0-8ee1-0cc049c85d75" "answer-3db0b808-399f-55d4-8d48-9e2b49e5368c" "answer-9a550f44-e8e6-5b84-9bae-f67384a3896a" "answer-d272b557-f219-5b4f-a4cc-433d4eed23a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-031a5be1-80e3-55f8-96a4-84489190a8dc" :answer/id #uuid "031a5be1-80e3-55f8-96a4-84489190a8dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67b84b0f-4bfb-52d0-8ee1-0cc049c85d75" :answer/id #uuid "67b84b0f-4bfb-52d0-8ee1-0cc049c85d75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3db0b808-399f-55d4-8d48-9e2b49e5368c" :answer/id #uuid "3db0b808-399f-55d4-8d48-9e2b49e5368c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a550f44-e8e6-5b84-9bae-f67384a3896a" :answer/id #uuid "9a550f44-e8e6-5b84-9bae-f67384a3896a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d272b557-f219-5b4f-a4cc-433d4eed23a7" :answer/id #uuid "d272b557-f219-5b4f-a4cc-433d4eed23a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1374041d-2f69-55e3-9b42-ce863bb1bab0" :question/id #uuid "1374041d-2f69-55e3-9b42-ce863bb1bab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146615" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,1)=0.77,\\,F(4,-1)=0.15,\\,F(1,1)=0.19,\\,F(1,-1)=0.09,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/01/01e04dfdac5f4abc16bd856dcfbe1b7361010cce2e7779658a33f293c1c52ae4.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2d2525118ca3b62c0829c0308fab168d0b806dfa13302c007a63cd354314d63f.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(1,1)+F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.77-0.15-0.19+0.09 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0973e0ae-8949-52fe-9d14-7a1a4925ba53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0973e0ae-8949-52fe-9d14-7a1a4925ba53" :answer-field/id #uuid "0973e0ae-8949-52fe-9d14-7a1a4925ba53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea93ddf2-c74d-5131-8f0c-698558bc1ccd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe0f133d-d936-51dc-8dad-167e59ac346b" "answer-05a1a33d-ed6a-57ac-b7c7-d80e7811473a" "answer-85b516c6-df16-5177-b323-6e0f55271ea3" "answer-ea93ddf2-c74d-5131-8f0c-698558bc1ccd" "answer-e248ad4f-2ddb-5621-a2c5-b027b720eeff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe0f133d-d936-51dc-8dad-167e59ac346b" :answer/id #uuid "fe0f133d-d936-51dc-8dad-167e59ac346b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.09" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05a1a33d-ed6a-57ac-b7c7-d80e7811473a" :answer/id #uuid "05a1a33d-ed6a-57ac-b7c7-d80e7811473a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.34" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85b516c6-df16-5177-b323-6e0f55271ea3" :answer/id #uuid "85b516c6-df16-5177-b323-6e0f55271ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea93ddf2-c74d-5131-8f0c-698558bc1ccd" :answer/id #uuid "ea93ddf2-c74d-5131-8f0c-698558bc1ccd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e248ad4f-2ddb-5621-a2c5-b027b720eeff" :answer/id #uuid "e248ad4f-2ddb-5621-a2c5-b027b720eeff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.61" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162413 :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.72,\\,F(10,6)=0.3,\\,F(8,9)=0.15,\\,F(8,6)=0.08,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(8<X\\le 10,6<Y\\le 9).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/04/045903aeef84fbd38d09e97f203d9169f0c1f10b662e343b82fc8113dbd1ecc3.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/81/8107e3dbe0cd180f8ce4ac8760f3fb341cb6d4accf1c698856aec361d4db23b7.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,6)$ and $(8,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,6).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8<X\\le 10,6<Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,6)-F(8,9)+F(8,6) \\\\  & =0.72-0.3-0.15+0.08 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159680 :answer-field/correct 17592186159684 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e2590f0-16cb-5122-9f2a-082c6b4b3553" :question/id #uuid "5e2590f0-16cb-5122-9f2a-082c6b4b3553" :question/math-academy-id "q-146624" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,3)=0.8,\\,F(1,3)=0,\\,F(4,1)=0.3,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/2b/2b159f4ba1873270f42b7afd2fdc4b969672c7d3cb7e694d519ab82dab33b283.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/e1/e186cf0e38e938798cdcd5bc10a5334230cc78cc77d114a14eba0c07702a1cdc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/eb/ebcfd8e0b553cab38a76eac77aab45186db92b3fe47a1fbb87a8623db4c29389.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,3)-F(4,1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2bf78da-ab1c-5f71-b728-dc9a284a8f6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2bf78da-ab1c-5f71-b728-dc9a284a8f6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bf78da-ab1c-5f71-b728-dc9a284a8f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f593ce60-a106-53c2-9747-5753576f33b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e47d4fb2-7dcf-5e0f-a66d-4a15f03bd60d" "answer-d9c5ab66-36db-5c3b-a383-38eec227f64a" "answer-effbaaf0-057d-535b-8f73-eca833f9ff0d" "answer-f593ce60-a106-53c2-9747-5753576f33b7" "answer-68889e05-7e66-53c4-a2df-e3cbdb4b7288"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e47d4fb2-7dcf-5e0f-a66d-4a15f03bd60d" :answer/id #uuid "e47d4fb2-7dcf-5e0f-a66d-4a15f03bd60d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9c5ab66-36db-5c3b-a383-38eec227f64a" :answer/id #uuid "d9c5ab66-36db-5c3b-a383-38eec227f64a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-effbaaf0-057d-535b-8f73-eca833f9ff0d" :answer/id #uuid "effbaaf0-057d-535b-8f73-eca833f9ff0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f593ce60-a106-53c2-9747-5753576f33b7" :answer/id #uuid "f593ce60-a106-53c2-9747-5753576f33b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68889e05-7e66-53c4-a2df-e3cbdb4b7288" :answer/id #uuid "68889e05-7e66-53c4-a2df-e3cbdb4b7288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fab237a1-fe76-5f6d-bb1c-9924690227b6" :question/id #uuid "fab237a1-fe76-5f6d-bb1c-9924690227b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146620" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(15,6)=0.82\\,F(12,6)=0.6\\,F(6,6)=0.12,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4dbaa771e897551b72f0c12ad2ba6940dfbef450c89815dc0f8912babd6a05.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fb/fb0624aa6e88d4811e77d3c38f24d1d20d201b8e7f6cfb23a3278afef794ab5d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.6-0.12 \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d52b8610-a26d-566a-a9f0-2b005b0ef722"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d52b8610-a26d-566a-a9f0-2b005b0ef722" :answer-field/id #uuid "d52b8610-a26d-566a-a9f0-2b005b0ef722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f19b40dc-ac21-5cf6-a61d-ef12a77f06e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8bf43fa-0a43-5b86-8a6b-461dae770a69" "answer-b285d0a5-dff5-5f04-93cf-71561d9480cb" "answer-f19b40dc-ac21-5cf6-a61d-ef12a77f06e0" "answer-3e0814a4-0dea-5ca4-b7fc-9b692b925da5" "answer-d9741b9a-5671-58f6-b927-57967f816283"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8bf43fa-0a43-5b86-8a6b-461dae770a69" :answer/id #uuid "e8bf43fa-0a43-5b86-8a6b-461dae770a69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b285d0a5-dff5-5f04-93cf-71561d9480cb" :answer/id #uuid "b285d0a5-dff5-5f04-93cf-71561d9480cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f19b40dc-ac21-5cf6-a61d-ef12a77f06e0" :answer/id #uuid "f19b40dc-ac21-5cf6-a61d-ef12a77f06e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e0814a4-0dea-5ca4-b7fc-9b692b925da5" :answer/id #uuid "3e0814a4-0dea-5ca4-b7fc-9b692b925da5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9741b9a-5671-58f6-b927-57967f816283" :answer/id #uuid "d9741b9a-5671-58f6-b927-57967f816283" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01cad121-bd37-5572-936b-c4e7143fa3ed" :question/id #uuid "01cad121-bd37-5572-936b-c4e7143fa3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-146843" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,6)=0.7,\\,F(9,6)=0.5,\\,F(6,6)=0.4,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>6,Y\\le 8).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/00/0036b6bd64900e2c6a0319f16788b2d563a8415256e2f21ba561b1fb41afdf34.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ and top-left corner at $(6,6),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/43/4394f404e54b8e585becb601f688eece3d4769efe2a124cb53b11d3432a9996b.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/5e/5ed86031d5762e3a2fe440fba52775fc6833243664fa18ee85c3449b50ff6cee.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6,Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.7-0.4 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7ab1ae6-237e-56d8-8f03-0612304e4446"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7ab1ae6-237e-56d8-8f03-0612304e4446" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ab1ae6-237e-56d8-8f03-0612304e4446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a66d5ce-9d44-5251-b536-627c7965cd9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-748fe1e3-e17b-53c4-8c73-d8e024db07c4" "answer-7ac31ebf-267d-559c-9469-a1debc88adc2" "answer-c6174c11-3ff7-5bae-977a-e4717bf36035" "answer-5a66d5ce-9d44-5251-b536-627c7965cd9b" "answer-ea1aeac0-6347-5581-9ef1-961cff54bddf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-748fe1e3-e17b-53c4-8c73-d8e024db07c4" :answer/id #uuid "748fe1e3-e17b-53c4-8c73-d8e024db07c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ac31ebf-267d-559c-9469-a1debc88adc2" :answer/id #uuid "7ac31ebf-267d-559c-9469-a1debc88adc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6174c11-3ff7-5bae-977a-e4717bf36035" :answer/id #uuid "c6174c11-3ff7-5bae-977a-e4717bf36035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a66d5ce-9d44-5251-b536-627c7965cd9b" :answer/id #uuid "5a66d5ce-9d44-5251-b536-627c7965cd9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea1aeac0-6347-5581-9ef1-961cff54bddf" :answer/id #uuid "ea1aeac0-6347-5581-9ef1-961cff54bddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd922824-164f-5af2-954c-b939958ff429" :question/id #uuid "fd922824-164f-5af2-954c-b939958ff429" :question/math-academy-id "q-146845" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,1)=0.67,\\,F(3,1)=0.43\\,F(1,1)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>3,Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb5c8084958ba3dc1175c1e92035d89fb4edcdf8ce333962b49f22b40552f71.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ and top-left corner at $(3,1),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/1c/1c28f3e2f820511a30aa64ccd3affacaa6d72e69da9c596827429b244bf1129a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,1)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/ae/ae993ae6e8c0d71a42ca0e11ed5b30a8d762b3fab12771247eca262f0131c4f5.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3,Y<2) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,1)-F(3,1) \\\\  & =0.67-0.43 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b5302b1-965f-57a3-8e52-58216fd37b2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b5302b1-965f-57a3-8e52-58216fd37b2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b5302b1-965f-57a3-8e52-58216fd37b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0f16e58-ba50-5519-b0c9-2c7d1935a069" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d933f6f-e2c3-51e2-80f4-1f904b3bbf7b" "answer-04ed47b4-e775-566f-bc2c-56a2a7bded91" "answer-e0f16e58-ba50-5519-b0c9-2c7d1935a069" "answer-9d89dae4-9bbc-5113-9c23-1d88b96505c0" "answer-be91de9f-ef83-54e9-8ed1-7d56afbe15c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d933f6f-e2c3-51e2-80f4-1f904b3bbf7b" :answer/id #uuid "7d933f6f-e2c3-51e2-80f4-1f904b3bbf7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.086" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ed47b4-e775-566f-bc2c-56a2a7bded91" :answer/id #uuid "04ed47b4-e775-566f-bc2c-56a2a7bded91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f16e58-ba50-5519-b0c9-2c7d1935a069" :answer/id #uuid "e0f16e58-ba50-5519-b0c9-2c7d1935a069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d89dae4-9bbc-5113-9c23-1d88b96505c0" :answer/id #uuid "9d89dae4-9bbc-5113-9c23-1d88b96505c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.47" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be91de9f-ef83-54e9-8ed1-7d56afbe15c5" :answer/id #uuid "be91de9f-ef83-54e9-8ed1-7d56afbe15c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.63" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ab8b5f0-f462-5316-91ff-26b04644c742" :question/id #uuid "9ab8b5f0-f462-5316-91ff-26b04644c742" :question/math-academy-id "q-146848" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.55,\\,F(10,3)=0.15,\\,F(4,9)=0.1,\\,F(4,3)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(5<X<11,4<Y<10).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/f4/f43c5ae8d27872c4aebe235c507600b7bf1806e22b52dd3b3ff2261509ac9925.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ and bottom-left corner at $(4,3)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/60/60bf5a958b682d513010c3eff9a8084072be62e286c0b0dfacb09960a3283e38.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,3)$ and $(4,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<11,4<Y<10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,3)-F(4,9)+F(4,3) \\\\  & =0.55-0.15-0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-23fa940f-fe5f-5cda-bceb-aaf37edf3942"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23fa940f-fe5f-5cda-bceb-aaf37edf3942" :answer-field/id #uuid "23fa940f-fe5f-5cda-bceb-aaf37edf3942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef6caa9c-a472-5218-9f51-793ff820cd6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87cd8823-38ea-50c5-94f7-78a13978c16c" "answer-76b02660-6e75-52ad-85ae-f40779aa5c78" "answer-ef6caa9c-a472-5218-9f51-793ff820cd6b" "answer-a704e947-f321-56a0-8e33-a84b3ace5f65" "answer-0270bd4d-b1c8-5184-b8a3-b42476109664"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87cd8823-38ea-50c5-94f7-78a13978c16c" :answer/id #uuid "87cd8823-38ea-50c5-94f7-78a13978c16c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76b02660-6e75-52ad-85ae-f40779aa5c78" :answer/id #uuid "76b02660-6e75-52ad-85ae-f40779aa5c78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef6caa9c-a472-5218-9f51-793ff820cd6b" :answer/id #uuid "ef6caa9c-a472-5218-9f51-793ff820cd6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a704e947-f321-56a0-8e33-a84b3ace5f65" :answer/id #uuid "a704e947-f321-56a0-8e33-a84b3ace5f65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0270bd4d-b1c8-5184-b8a3-b42476109664" :answer/id #uuid "0270bd4d-b1c8-5184-b8a3-b42476109664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d10653a4-2ac5-5be4-9b7e-7dbc6ddafa84" :question/id #uuid "d10653a4-2ac5-5be4-9b7e-7dbc6ddafa84" :question/math-academy-id "q-146849" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,1)=0.7,\\,F(1,-2)=0.3,\\,F(-2,1)=0.4,\\,F(-2,-2)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/ae/ae7d480e52257825e679349ce185b4aa08ed3b096fd7a780ece56c90439798fe.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(1,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(-2,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcb1b0b518485dfcd4066a0259fea4a2dc050be47e95b4de0b925d8f1c997aa.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(1,-2)$ and $(-2,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.5\\le X\\le 1.5,-1.5\\le Y\\le 1.5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(1,1)-F(1,-2)-F(-2,1)+F(-2,-2) \\\\  & =0.7-0.3-0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04cedd67-eefd-5664-9e79-71d0a4cd5a23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04cedd67-eefd-5664-9e79-71d0a4cd5a23" :answer-field/id #uuid "04cedd67-eefd-5664-9e79-71d0a4cd5a23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94a56b5a-a18a-5d96-95cb-482e725c227c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94a56b5a-a18a-5d96-95cb-482e725c227c" "answer-5b89b74d-4e89-5bba-9fe9-101256cc15c4" "answer-4231ab6f-77a8-55e4-aea1-903e92ce3f2b" "answer-2734458c-e754-5af7-aae6-6e7c43061d34" "answer-7dc746bf-7a23-524c-8698-422890fb3fae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94a56b5a-a18a-5d96-95cb-482e725c227c" :answer/id #uuid "94a56b5a-a18a-5d96-95cb-482e725c227c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b89b74d-4e89-5bba-9fe9-101256cc15c4" :answer/id #uuid "5b89b74d-4e89-5bba-9fe9-101256cc15c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4231ab6f-77a8-55e4-aea1-903e92ce3f2b" :answer/id #uuid "4231ab6f-77a8-55e4-aea1-903e92ce3f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2734458c-e754-5af7-aae6-6e7c43061d34" :answer/id #uuid "2734458c-e754-5af7-aae6-6e7c43061d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dc746bf-7a23-524c-8698-422890fb3fae" :answer/id #uuid "7dc746bf-7a23-524c-8698-422890fb3fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8dece31e-c114-5d98-b9c8-904ef5ba8d5d" :question/id #uuid "8dece31e-c114-5d98-b9c8-904ef5ba8d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146851" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,3)=0.6,\\,F(5,1)=0.23,\\,F(2,3)=0.32,\\,F(2,1)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2dd2929b39c9d0357516ebea9f1f31ed2b5f64c6154184ec904996c51162c144.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(5,3)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/db/db601c0b0d2931a61d2218e196ea7485fc8d258cb77c604dc44661687de43beb.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,3).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,3)-F(5,1)-F(2,3)+F(2,1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.23-0.32+0.05 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad0272b4-dd5a-544a-a7d4-a49f4c49e16a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad0272b4-dd5a-544a-a7d4-a49f4c49e16a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0272b4-dd5a-544a-a7d4-a49f4c49e16a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6cfd97d-9e66-5650-8d9b-e6f83d8cc114" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58bac764-44f8-55c1-ba07-ab0f55dee029" "answer-b6cfd97d-9e66-5650-8d9b-e6f83d8cc114" "answer-afcef1f2-ba8a-55d4-b054-349a510940db" "answer-e32a7cb9-b939-581c-bc82-d395bf018748" "answer-d5817d11-103f-57c6-b7df-8777bf2f338b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58bac764-44f8-55c1-ba07-ab0f55dee029" :answer/id #uuid "58bac764-44f8-55c1-ba07-ab0f55dee029" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6cfd97d-9e66-5650-8d9b-e6f83d8cc114" :answer/id #uuid "b6cfd97d-9e66-5650-8d9b-e6f83d8cc114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afcef1f2-ba8a-55d4-b054-349a510940db" :answer/id #uuid "afcef1f2-ba8a-55d4-b054-349a510940db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e32a7cb9-b939-581c-bc82-d395bf018748" :answer/id #uuid "e32a7cb9-b939-581c-bc82-d395bf018748" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5817d11-103f-57c6-b7df-8777bf2f338b" :answer/id #uuid "d5817d11-103f-57c6-b7df-8777bf2f338b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163341 :tutorial/content "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X,$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P( X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)  = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are the **supports** of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are $S_X = \\{ 0, 1 \\}$ and $S_Y = \\{ 0, 1, 2 \\},$ respectively. \n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1).$ By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.3+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163360 :tutorial/content "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\n \n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1).\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1.$\n\n![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty).$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F( \\infty, y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162165 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186062761 :answer-field/choices 17592186062762] [:db/retract 17592186062761 :answer-field/choices 17592186062763] [:db/retract 17592186062761 :answer-field/choices 17592186062764] [:db/retract 17592186062761 :answer-field/choices 17592186062765] [:db/retract 17592186062761 :answer-field/choices 17592186062766] {:db/id 17592186064214 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c4a8c11-58ca-529c-a377-f348add911e4" "question-dfaf6e99-1d8f-5b5e-92bd-9fd3c571d232"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063984 :question/problem "Simplify $-3t^3\\left(3t^2+2t\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t) & = \\\\ (-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t) & = \\\\ ((-3)\\cdot 3)({t}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{2})+((-3)\\cdot 2)({t}^{3}\\cdot t) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{3+2})+(-6)({t}^{3+1}) & = \\\\ (-9)({t}^{5})+(-6)({t}^{4}) & = \\\\ -9{t}^{5}-6{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064215 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b6d8c42-1d59-52b0-9958-cceeca4a3dbe" "question-02b8d95c-cd10-56fd-a2ee-19a46309a688"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063985 :question/problem "Simplify $-5x\\left(x^3-4\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x({x}^{3}-4) & = \\\\ (-5x)({x}^{3})+(-5x)(-4) & = \\\\ (-5)(x\\cdot {x}^{3})+((-5)\\cdot (-4))(x) & = \\\\ (-5){x}^{1+3}+(20)x & = \\\\ -5{x}^{4}+20x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064216 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a358c984-11c0-53e4-9e16-174bbfcc06eb" "question-526966d4-b472-58c0-be00-2ffec30b650e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063986 :question/problem "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) & = \\\\ ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) & = \\\\ -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064217 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d5a3382-d5a0-5b4b-ac58-85cbe16d22b9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063987 :question/problem "Simplify $xy\\left(3x^2+xy+4\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy(3{x}^{2}+xy+4) & = \\\\ (xy)\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+(xy)\\cdot (xy)+(xy)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 3(x\\cdot {x}^{2})(y)+(x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y)+4(xy) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3})(y)+({x}^{2})({y}^{2})+4xy & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c4a8c11-58ca-529c-a377-f348add911e4" :question/id #uuid "1c4a8c11-58ca-529c-a377-f348add911e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216986" :question/problem "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$2{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}+4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}+4) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{4})+2{x}^{2}\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ 6{x}^{2+4}+8{x}^{2} & = \\\\ 6{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5587de01-f6eb-59f8-8ec0-eb041dd8281c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5587de01-f6eb-59f8-8ec0-eb041dd8281c" :answer-field/id #uuid "5587de01-f6eb-59f8-8ec0-eb041dd8281c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7f23fdbd-3fd3-56f8-9056-aa6d59dc3eab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f23fdbd-3fd3-56f8-9056-aa6d59dc3eab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f23fdbd-3fd3-56f8-9056-aa6d59dc3eab" :answer/id #uuid "7f23fdbd-3fd3-56f8-9056-aa6d59dc3eab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6x^{6}+8x^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfaf6e99-1d8f-5b5e-92bd-9fd3c571d232" :question/id #uuid "dfaf6e99-1d8f-5b5e-92bd-9fd3c571d232" :question/math-academy-id "q-44642" :question/problem "$-2{w}^{3}({w}^{3}+3w+2)=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{w}^{3}({w}^{3}+3w+2) & = \\\\ (-2{w}^{3})({w}^{3})+(-2{w}^{3})(3w)+(-2{w}^{3})(2) & = \\\\ (-2)({w}^{3}\\cdot {w}^{3})+((-2)\\cdot 3)({w}^{3}\\cdot w)+((-2)\\cdot 2)({w}^{3}) & = \\\\ (-2){w}^{3+3}+(-6){w}^{3+1}+(-4){w}^{3} & = \\\\ -2{w}^{6}-6{w}^{4}-4{w}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ea4164d-199b-5fa8-988a-88bdda69d024"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ea4164d-199b-5fa8-988a-88bdda69d024" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea4164d-199b-5fa8-988a-88bdda69d024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38f7e8e6-c1c7-508e-8d6a-3fd5596d3fe5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f860bb7-7c51-532a-820b-6f6498f5e522" "answer-f9b42dee-7635-536d-b190-e1e49a5af093" "answer-f7876760-d7ee-5fa6-8f80-c522e35eda1d" "answer-ca26b375-4a8c-5ae3-b1e9-dd7d05125d32" "answer-38f7e8e6-c1c7-508e-8d6a-3fd5596d3fe5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f860bb7-7c51-532a-820b-6f6498f5e522" :answer/id #uuid "7f860bb7-7c51-532a-820b-6f6498f5e522" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{w}^{6}+3w+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9b42dee-7635-536d-b190-e1e49a5af093" :answer/id #uuid "f9b42dee-7635-536d-b190-e1e49a5af093" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{w}^{6}-3{w}^{4}-2{w}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7876760-d7ee-5fa6-8f80-c522e35eda1d" :answer/id #uuid "f7876760-d7ee-5fa6-8f80-c522e35eda1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{w}^{6}-6{w}^{4}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca26b375-4a8c-5ae3-b1e9-dd7d05125d32" :answer/id #uuid "ca26b375-4a8c-5ae3-b1e9-dd7d05125d32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{w}^{6}-6{w}^{4}+4{w}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38f7e8e6-c1c7-508e-8d6a-3fd5596d3fe5" :answer/id #uuid "38f7e8e6-c1c7-508e-8d6a-3fd5596d3fe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{w}^{6}-6{w}^{4}-4{w}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b6d8c42-1d59-52b0-9958-cceeca4a3dbe" :question/id #uuid "0b6d8c42-1d59-52b0-9958-cceeca4a3dbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-216998" :question/problem "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$-{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}-x+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}(3{x}^{4}-x+2) & = \\\\ (-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{4})+(-{x}^{2})(-x)+(-{x}^{2})2 & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4})+((-1)\\cdot (-1))({x}^{2}\\cdot x)+((-1)\\cdot 2)({x}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-3){x}^{2+4}+(1){x}^{2+1}+(-2){x}^{2} & = \\\\ -3{x}^{6}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d01f0664-679f-5f38-857f-275bf91d4556"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d01f0664-679f-5f38-857f-275bf91d4556" :answer-field/id #uuid "d01f0664-679f-5f38-857f-275bf91d4556" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eee98293-3395-5eb0-b143-f256c73f4a75" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eee98293-3395-5eb0-b143-f256c73f4a75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eee98293-3395-5eb0-b143-f256c73f4a75" :answer/id #uuid "eee98293-3395-5eb0-b143-f256c73f4a75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3x^{6}+x^{3}-2x^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02b8d95c-cd10-56fd-a2ee-19a46309a688" :question/id #uuid "02b8d95c-cd10-56fd-a2ee-19a46309a688" :question/math-academy-id "q-215318" :question/problem "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$-{x}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-5x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-5x) & = \\\\ (-{x}^{3})(2{x}^{2})+(-{x}^{3})(-5x) & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 2)({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-1)\\cdot (-5))({x}^{3}\\cdot x) & = \\\\ (-2){x}^{3+2}+(5){x}^{3+1} & = \\\\ -2{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73fd6360-f6ac-52b2-8f2e-d34673fe83c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73fd6360-f6ac-52b2-8f2e-d34673fe83c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "73fd6360-f6ac-52b2-8f2e-d34673fe83c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2affc424-b64d-5e66-8ac0-e677197618b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2affc424-b64d-5e66-8ac0-e677197618b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2affc424-b64d-5e66-8ac0-e677197618b5" :answer/id #uuid "2affc424-b64d-5e66-8ac0-e677197618b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2x^{5}+5x^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064599 :question/problem "$2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3})=${{field-1}}\n$\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${b}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{3}(-\\frac{b}{4}+{b}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ 2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{4})+2{b}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}+2{b}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})({b}^{3+1})+2({b}^{3+3})+(-\\frac{2}{3}){b}^{3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}+2{b}^{6}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3} & = \\\\ 2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061696 :answer-field/correct "answer-837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer-field/choices ["answer-837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/id #uuid "837f91b7-1b58-545a-96dd-2f97327fe650" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{b}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061697 :answer-field/correct "answer-d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/id #uuid "d8e4d1a5-111b-5483-8b45-254f42d5e582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a358c984-11c0-53e4-9e16-174bbfcc06eb" :question/id #uuid "a358c984-11c0-53e4-9e16-174bbfcc06eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-217022" :question/problem "Expand the following product. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}a)(9{a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}a)(-15a) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9)({a}^{1}\\cdot {a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-15))(a\\cdot a) & = \\\\ (3)({a}^{1+4})+(-5)({a}^{1+1}) & = \\\\ 3{a}^{5}-5{a}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e0d39888-088e-5d8c-98c5-3147b816b4d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0d39888-088e-5d8c-98c5-3147b816b4d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d39888-088e-5d8c-98c5-3147b816b4d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dedb42c4-e00d-5bfa-8c63-1f4242d6fe90" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dedb42c4-e00d-5bfa-8c63-1f4242d6fe90"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dedb42c4-e00d-5bfa-8c63-1f4242d6fe90" :answer/id #uuid "dedb42c4-e00d-5bfa-8c63-1f4242d6fe90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3a^{5}-5a^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-526966d4-b472-58c0-be00-2ffec30b650e" :question/id #uuid "526966d4-b472-58c0-be00-2ffec30b650e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74584" :question/problem "$6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) & = \\\\ (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{x}{2})+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) & = \\\\ (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{2}x)+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot x)+(6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}))({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (3)({x}^{3+1})+(-2)\\cdot ({x}^{3+3}) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-711cfcc8-2acd-5237-b383-a491ba95980c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-711cfcc8-2acd-5237-b383-a491ba95980c" :answer-field/id #uuid "711cfcc8-2acd-5237-b383-a491ba95980c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9939d3e9-7f4e-5cc1-9753-721ee0a8d618" "answer-eccfdde5-d210-5907-b9d1-5dcc7cc45b70" "answer-cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" "answer-e6b65738-d03d-56a9-a07a-8e4b68fc5c09" "answer-7e4ba564-c701-5756-89b9-c57ee635a3fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9939d3e9-7f4e-5cc1-9753-721ee0a8d618" :answer/id #uuid "9939d3e9-7f4e-5cc1-9753-721ee0a8d618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eccfdde5-d210-5907-b9d1-5dcc7cc45b70" :answer/id #uuid "eccfdde5-d210-5907-b9d1-5dcc7cc45b70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" :answer/id #uuid "cf656d9b-75ef-554a-9036-89079b6ca3d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6b65738-d03d-56a9-a07a-8e4b68fc5c09" :answer/id #uuid "e6b65738-d03d-56a9-a07a-8e4b68fc5c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e4ba564-c701-5756-89b9-c57ee635a3fa" :answer/id #uuid "7e4ba564-c701-5756-89b9-c57ee635a3fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064784 :question/problem "$-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5})=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{p}^{4}(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (-{p}^{4})(-7{p}^{5}{q}^{2})+(-{p}^{4})(3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (\\,(-1)\\cdot (-7)\\,)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{5})({q}^{2})+(\\,(-1)\\cdot 3)({p}^{4}\\cdot {p}^{2})({q}^{5}) & = \\\\ (7)({p}^{4+5})({q}^{2})+(-3)({p}^{4+2})({q}^{5}) & = \\\\ 7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062761 :answer-field/correct "answer-88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-109e6613-6e55-5b3c-b18a-2c3b10925fdd" "answer-bc3aa44a-8098-5049-9b4d-ccf6de1b1ce3" "answer-cfc92d42-9d4c-57e8-aebe-b1e85b358dfe" "answer-d657ff47-0060-560a-974e-93ba3c676167" "answer-88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-109e6613-6e55-5b3c-b18a-2c3b10925fdd" :answer/id #uuid "109e6613-6e55-5b3c-b18a-2c3b10925fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc3aa44a-8098-5049-9b4d-ccf6de1b1ce3" :answer/id #uuid "bc3aa44a-8098-5049-9b4d-ccf6de1b1ce3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfc92d42-9d4c-57e8-aebe-b1e85b358dfe" :answer/id #uuid "cfc92d42-9d4c-57e8-aebe-b1e85b358dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{2}{q}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d657ff47-0060-560a-974e-93ba3c676167" :answer/id #uuid "d657ff47-0060-560a-974e-93ba3c676167" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}+3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de" :answer/id #uuid "88bfa841-9ff2-5a6a-8e9a-04cdcb7136de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7{p}^{9}{q}^{2}-3{p}^{6}{q}^{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d5a3382-d5a0-5b4b-ac58-85cbe16d22b9" :question/id #uuid "5d5a3382-d5a0-5b4b-ac58-85cbe16d22b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-44739" :question/problem "$-4{a}^{3}(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2}+3{a}^{2}+2)=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{a}^{3}(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2}+3{a}^{2}+2) & = \\\\ (-4{a}^{3})(2{a}^{4}{b}^{2})+(-4{a}^{3})(3{a}^{2})+(-4{a}^{3})(2) & = \\\\ (-8)({a}^{3+4})({b}^{2})+(-12)({a}^{3+2})+(-8)({a}^{3}) & = \\\\ -8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}-12{a}^{5}-8{a}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3696d071-f0d3-5573-a881-67ffa31234d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3696d071-f0d3-5573-a881-67ffa31234d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "3696d071-f0d3-5573-a881-67ffa31234d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84220bc2-e531-5237-87af-5c809e3282d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-188d64d7-f5a9-5b3d-b319-60d93b600e27" "answer-a51afb8d-99ae-5fe9-9770-5fb4ba65fc0d" "answer-3533f1c5-9908-5683-a6c0-66c0a80238a5" "answer-84220bc2-e531-5237-87af-5c809e3282d9" "answer-f164b081-8957-5f0a-ae3c-8022dc893cac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-188d64d7-f5a9-5b3d-b319-60d93b600e27" :answer/id #uuid "188d64d7-f5a9-5b3d-b319-60d93b600e27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8{a}^{12}{b}^{2}-12{a}^{6}b-8{a}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a51afb8d-99ae-5fe9-9770-5fb4ba65fc0d" :answer/id #uuid "a51afb8d-99ae-5fe9-9770-5fb4ba65fc0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}+3{a}^{2}b+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3533f1c5-9908-5683-a6c0-66c0a80238a5" :answer/id #uuid "3533f1c5-9908-5683-a6c0-66c0a80238a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}-12{a}^{5}+2{a}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84220bc2-e531-5237-87af-5c809e3282d9" :answer/id #uuid "84220bc2-e531-5237-87af-5c809e3282d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}-12{a}^{5}-8{a}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f164b081-8957-5f0a-ae3c-8022dc893cac" :answer/id #uuid "f164b081-8957-5f0a-ae3c-8022dc893cac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{a}^{7}{b}^{2}+12{a}^{5}+8{a}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065439 :tutorial/content "To expand an expression like   \n\n![](images/cd/cd1928f00c75236e8749153ea82e10efd3fc88d435eb095a940215337086e8a9.svg)  \n\nwe can use the distributive law. We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:  \n\n![](images/22/225564303e86ce4bce242c66c78434d94114de43c144933d6cadda2b718cfbc9.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064439 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186321058 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/id #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35293" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" "answer-9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" "answer-86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" "answer-8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" "answer-bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/id #uuid "04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/id #uuid "9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/id #uuid "86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/id #uuid "8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/id #uuid "bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120530 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=4$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ (not $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119294 :answer-field/correct 17592186119295 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186197912 :answer-field/choices 17592186197913] [:db/retract 17592186197912 :answer-field/choices 17592186197914] [:db/retract 17592186197912 :answer-field/choices 17592186197915] [:db/retract 17592186197912 :answer-field/choices 17592186197916] {:db/id 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" "question-e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" "question-5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" "question-ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" "question-fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198235 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/e9/e9a07b75be73621bb23edb6f4da67999c5f5ce07b8776f549cf12464e74fa702.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/a1/a1228e77194b92d8b2c527845273ae9f70d9b90d4c3091f5ed9dd9f8c430776c.svg)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" "question-7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" "question-ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" "question-c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198015 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/99/99eef26d2de27b8ae6f8cf872b352368efaf2703e47bcea6e406478db14d6e18.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7d00c49df06196f76846eeee5152f258a53c4936b51285233531872906ced6b.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" "question-b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" "question-e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" "question-4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" "question-03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198016 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d526a4897f071f523b5900c55fd8797cf5e4270a3031270013e54dc9dca9ca4.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/ae/aeb4cdc398b9f13551ecea013fef9ea607786ab1f9d3466da846dd10b930f378.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" :question/id #uuid "d52198ba-3323-5776-a083-de931434cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-82798" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-912cb459-ddba-5110-ba83-e5c7cb9c0fab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-912cb459-ddba-5110-ba83-e5c7cb9c0fab" :answer-field/id #uuid "912cb459-ddba-5110-ba83-e5c7cb9c0fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8271e591-b018-5489-b0eb-360dc3d744e9" "answer-e3cd4b6c-e4fe-5b75-be7e-3479440b02e3" "answer-538437b0-2f11-5cf6-a513-3b5f95549b2e" "answer-7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" "answer-6ec46383-2d2f-5cde-b1c3-b222f3cbe2e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8271e591-b018-5489-b0eb-360dc3d744e9" :answer/id #uuid "8271e591-b018-5489-b0eb-360dc3d744e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(2u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3cd4b6c-e4fe-5b75-be7e-3479440b02e3" :answer/id #uuid "e3cd4b6c-e4fe-5b75-be7e-3479440b02e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-538437b0-2f11-5cf6-a513-3b5f95549b2e" :answer/id #uuid "538437b0-2f11-5cf6-a513-3b5f95549b2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/id #uuid "7b7952ce-f2f3-57b0-b782-9aa012888b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ec46383-2d2f-5cde-b1c3-b222f3cbe2e9" :answer/id #uuid "6ec46383-2d2f-5cde-b1c3-b222f3cbe2e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" :question/id #uuid "e0897fe5-68e3-5373-b337-c33b7a65cd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-82818" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ab7f9c1-3d45-58a6-bbb6-a63febcef757"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ab7f9c1-3d45-58a6-bbb6-a63febcef757" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab7f9c1-3d45-58a6-bbb6-a63febcef757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b71f8dcb-5c9d-54b5-b09b-deeeea423e1a" "answer-50d5f25b-3bff-52d9-b4d2-40d73df06c36" "answer-2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" "answer-844f29d7-a7a5-5823-a2f4-5f8ba38566ff" "answer-632142b3-3dde-53ef-9fc3-71eb83cdcd38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b71f8dcb-5c9d-54b5-b09b-deeeea423e1a" :answer/id #uuid "b71f8dcb-5c9d-54b5-b09b-deeeea423e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50d5f25b-3bff-52d9-b4d2-40d73df06c36" :answer/id #uuid "50d5f25b-3bff-52d9-b4d2-40d73df06c36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/id #uuid "2fed0bd2-6515-5d71-9deb-11c96d0f5a24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-844f29d7-a7a5-5823-a2f4-5f8ba38566ff" :answer/id #uuid "844f29d7-a7a5-5823-a2f4-5f8ba38566ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-632142b3-3dde-53ef-9fc3-71eb83cdcd38" :answer/id #uuid "632142b3-3dde-53ef-9fc3-71eb83cdcd38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" :question/id #uuid "5de90e63-3afe-527a-b599-3cdcd703aa52" :question/math-academy-id "q-83656" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u={e}^{x}$ and $v={e}^{y},$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le {e}^{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le {e}^{y}\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "f081c215-96de-53ac-b301-926b90ac00ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f4c86f2-5cfb-5693-a142-edc6be59008a" "answer-95eca681-53e5-51f4-8569-95f9733c2dbe" "answer-68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" "answer-79ad7697-010f-568d-851d-4911a16f499a" "answer-344eed9f-15e2-5d9d-a328-322b0adeb917"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f4c86f2-5cfb-5693-a142-edc6be59008a" :answer/id #uuid "0f4c86f2-5cfb-5693-a142-edc6be59008a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95eca681-53e5-51f4-8569-95f9733c2dbe" :answer/id #uuid "95eca681-53e5-51f4-8569-95f9733c2dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/id #uuid "68632396-ccfe-581e-8589-6ce419497b94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79ad7697-010f-568d-851d-4911a16f499a" :answer/id #uuid "79ad7697-010f-568d-851d-4911a16f499a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-344eed9f-15e2-5d9d-a328-322b0adeb917" :answer/id #uuid "344eed9f-15e2-5d9d-a328-322b0adeb917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" :question/id #uuid "ea750ce2-b7b3-5ba2-80fa-7d5a87470671" :question/math-academy-id "q-160850" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b345d81-65d6-5a3f-9150-c97ad1b7b016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" "answer-91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" "answer-dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" "answer-6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" "answer-fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" :answer/id #uuid "8236c364-9a58-5bd5-bca8-e007796586fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" :answer/id #uuid "91e046c8-5b65-5972-9eef-cd8f897a8cd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" :answer/id #uuid "dbc70cb2-b436-5aca-b9d7-fabe9b0e5de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" :answer/id #uuid "6364af2a-3d6b-5851-9049-3595e8cfce71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/id #uuid "fc8d0347-5303-50ad-8ea6-72f1ba59d8dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d" :question/id #uuid "fee52d9d-67c4-5104-9f57-4122f892110d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82911" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x-y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7505a4fd-3c5e-5a38-a1f7-3891a3c2965c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7505a4fd-3c5e-5a38-a1f7-3891a3c2965c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7505a4fd-3c5e-5a38-a1f7-3891a3c2965c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8273dafa-bda1-5a66-9cff-9b7575876a40" "answer-ec2a5b9c-1081-52b7-b89e-e7f3524f0dee" "answer-bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" "answer-e59d2e21-5875-56c6-8566-91c968ba9e4a" "answer-0e55ce08-51f3-5f7d-9882-1ad73c59bda5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8273dafa-bda1-5a66-9cff-9b7575876a40" :answer/id #uuid "8273dafa-bda1-5a66-9cff-9b7575876a40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec2a5b9c-1081-52b7-b89e-e7f3524f0dee" :answer/id #uuid "ec2a5b9c-1081-52b7-b89e-e7f3524f0dee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/id #uuid "bcd0ae49-abbe-5447-a383-493cedc60a77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e59d2e21-5875-56c6-8566-91c968ba9e4a" :answer/id #uuid "e59d2e21-5875-56c6-8566-91c968ba9e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e55ce08-51f3-5f7d-9882-1ad73c59bda5" :answer/id #uuid "0e55ce08-51f3-5f7d-9882-1ad73c59bda5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" :question/id #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" "answer-dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" "answer-1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" "answer-5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" "answer-bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" :answer/id #uuid "dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" :answer/id #uuid "1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" :answer/id #uuid "5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c" :answer/id #uuid "bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" :question/id #uuid "7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83653" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a913f95c-4f3f-51d9-a609-d167776b85b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a913f95c-4f3f-51d9-a609-d167776b85b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "a913f95c-4f3f-51d9-a609-d167776b85b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ac193e9-56da-5e90-afd1-e67c5f8d1870" "answer-f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" "answer-8be6f850-b0e1-5bd0-adfc-a35ba9264b99" "answer-b6110701-1956-577b-a189-4d9681a4e3ce" "answer-602828b8-7fa4-51b0-ae3e-ca2c02d7c8af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ac193e9-56da-5e90-afd1-e67c5f8d1870" :answer/id #uuid "2ac193e9-56da-5e90-afd1-e67c5f8d1870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/id #uuid "f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8be6f850-b0e1-5bd0-adfc-a35ba9264b99" :answer/id #uuid "8be6f850-b0e1-5bd0-adfc-a35ba9264b99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6110701-1956-577b-a189-4d9681a4e3ce" :answer/id #uuid "b6110701-1956-577b-a189-4d9681a4e3ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-602828b8-7fa4-51b0-ae3e-ca2c02d7c8af" :answer/id #uuid "602828b8-7fa4-51b0-ae3e-ca2c02d7c8af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" :question/id #uuid "ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160918" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e6427b8-37d4-5988-9502-b3fd4454d9b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e6427b8-37d4-5988-9502-b3fd4454d9b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6427b8-37d4-5988-9502-b3fd4454d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff742366-fc6b-51c9-9bb1-c3bf559e492f" "answer-c8d2beaf-f051-5e9a-a9bb-d9e392012d19" "answer-78a5344d-2607-521a-bc5c-b422c76ae208" "answer-71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" "answer-e262d3c6-319b-5ade-8d21-fe6baf79d69d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff742366-fc6b-51c9-9bb1-c3bf559e492f" :answer/id #uuid "ff742366-fc6b-51c9-9bb1-c3bf559e492f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8d2beaf-f051-5e9a-a9bb-d9e392012d19" :answer/id #uuid "c8d2beaf-f051-5e9a-a9bb-d9e392012d19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4e-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78a5344d-2607-521a-bc5c-b422c76ae208" :answer/id #uuid "78a5344d-2607-521a-bc5c-b422c76ae208" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/id #uuid "71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e262d3c6-319b-5ade-8d21-fe6baf79d69d" :answer/id #uuid "e262d3c6-319b-5ade-8d21-fe6baf79d69d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4(e-1)}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" :question/id #uuid "c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" :question/math-academy-id "q-38309" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be68da14-ad39-5dd8-8b81-96f9bc5c3b1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be68da14-ad39-5dd8-8b81-96f9bc5c3b1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "be68da14-ad39-5dd8-8b81-96f9bc5c3b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-062b1938-49ed-576d-84ed-09a8fb852cc9" "answer-9d25158d-398d-5c30-8fda-c6ddce1b0d76" "answer-34aac4ef-6b3d-5a11-9211-3216b95b6351" "answer-f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" "answer-365aa022-a66e-5c42-b417-e96430223091"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-062b1938-49ed-576d-84ed-09a8fb852cc9" :answer/id #uuid "062b1938-49ed-576d-84ed-09a8fb852cc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d25158d-398d-5c30-8fda-c6ddce1b0d76" :answer/id #uuid "9d25158d-398d-5c30-8fda-c6ddce1b0d76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34aac4ef-6b3d-5a11-9211-3216b95b6351" :answer/id #uuid "34aac4ef-6b3d-5a11-9211-3216b95b6351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/id #uuid "f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-365aa022-a66e-5c42-b417-e96430223091" :answer/id #uuid "365aa022-a66e-5c42-b417-e96430223091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199007 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197912 :answer-field/correct "answer-4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" "answer-69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" "answer-4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" "answer-50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/id #uuid "a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/id #uuid "69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-e}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/id #uuid "50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" :question/id #uuid "d3e35845-58c1-5603-a038-b92edbbc2e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-160906" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-848b178b-00cd-5964-8724-b50b402d0b68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-848b178b-00cd-5964-8724-b50b402d0b68" :answer-field/id #uuid "848b178b-00cd-5964-8724-b50b402d0b68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1761c3e4-c91a-57a8-9013-9b3654a234fa" "answer-ae180a90-3ca2-5c61-ac30-22916f24dea1" "answer-47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" "answer-e5aa3207-9a4b-5442-a65a-773ebcb88f61" "answer-f081f9d6-ce8e-5bb4-8583-a147cbb0c2be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1761c3e4-c91a-57a8-9013-9b3654a234fa" :answer/id #uuid "1761c3e4-c91a-57a8-9013-9b3654a234fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae180a90-3ca2-5c61-ac30-22916f24dea1" :answer/id #uuid "ae180a90-3ca2-5c61-ac30-22916f24dea1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" :answer/id #uuid "47c7196f-be5c-55f4-aa25-27610bf7736c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5aa3207-9a4b-5442-a65a-773ebcb88f61" :answer/id #uuid "e5aa3207-9a4b-5442-a65a-773ebcb88f61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f081f9d6-ce8e-5bb4-8583-a147cbb0c2be" :answer/id #uuid "f081f9d6-ce8e-5bb4-8583-a147cbb0c2be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" :question/id #uuid "b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-160938" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14aeda53-bc0f-5285-a152-aac2c1de7b55" "answer-31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" "answer-11e03497-2478-5699-b8c8-60dc8393efd3" "answer-328e9ba1-7166-5fcf-9c18-bf5334f7dc30" "answer-c05027eb-9d67-552f-950c-d7aa5524cbba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14aeda53-bc0f-5285-a152-aac2c1de7b55" :answer/id #uuid "14aeda53-bc0f-5285-a152-aac2c1de7b55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/id #uuid "31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11e03497-2478-5699-b8c8-60dc8393efd3" :answer/id #uuid "11e03497-2478-5699-b8c8-60dc8393efd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-328e9ba1-7166-5fcf-9c18-bf5334f7dc30" :answer/id #uuid "328e9ba1-7166-5fcf-9c18-bf5334f7dc30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c05027eb-9d67-552f-950c-d7aa5524cbba" :answer/id #uuid "c05027eb-9d67-552f-950c-d7aa5524cbba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" :question/id #uuid "e4b188aa-dc23-5bc9-90f6-b5a36b6d8b4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160902" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-1),$ $(2,0),$ $(3,-2),$ and $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f7c61ba-8de7-54e4-9c9a-0d1e7cbb1333"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f7c61ba-8de7-54e4-9c9a-0d1e7cbb1333" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7c61ba-8de7-54e4-9c9a-0d1e7cbb1333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" "answer-7e36322d-c7bd-5d73-a142-eb2532e6eb77" "answer-73ea0adf-89e3-52c6-a0cd-0e6c3a1d6e20" "answer-f372503b-18dc-5dbe-8608-da48adbf20c1" "answer-f07c8233-f2b2-5d87-8d57-e59421760285"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" :answer/id #uuid "db5ef865-a8de-5c3e-835e-5fc660d5d30b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{105}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e36322d-c7bd-5d73-a142-eb2532e6eb77" :answer/id #uuid "7e36322d-c7bd-5d73-a142-eb2532e6eb77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73ea0adf-89e3-52c6-a0cd-0e6c3a1d6e20" :answer/id #uuid "73ea0adf-89e3-52c6-a0cd-0e6c3a1d6e20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f372503b-18dc-5dbe-8608-da48adbf20c1" :answer/id #uuid "f372503b-18dc-5dbe-8608-da48adbf20c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f07c8233-f2b2-5d87-8d57-e59421760285" :answer/id #uuid "f07c8233-f2b2-5d87-8d57-e59421760285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" :question/id #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" :question/math-academy-id "q-160903" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" "answer-16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" "answer-943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" "answer-96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" "answer-09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" :answer/id #uuid "16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" :answer/id #uuid "943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" :answer/id #uuid "96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5" :answer/id #uuid "09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" :question/id #uuid "03e70789-b720-5486-ac24-d7c0b4013554" :question/math-academy-id "q-160937" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-149fcd69-9998-58e5-a043-dbc9ef09dd8b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-149fcd69-9998-58e5-a043-dbc9ef09dd8b" :answer-field/id #uuid "149fcd69-9998-58e5-a043-dbc9ef09dd8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbe6534a-9156-5f3a-a3f4-adabfe81028f" "answer-e902cbe7-031f-5540-a22a-6e30754fbd91" "answer-cc1e541a-a0b5-50e1-8a46-d0f83df1d6c4" "answer-3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" "answer-30fd4f57-3f0a-5ebc-aa07-bb84afb9cc7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbe6534a-9156-5f3a-a3f4-adabfe81028f" :answer/id #uuid "fbe6534a-9156-5f3a-a3f4-adabfe81028f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e902cbe7-031f-5540-a22a-6e30754fbd91" :answer/id #uuid "e902cbe7-031f-5540-a22a-6e30754fbd91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc1e541a-a0b5-50e1-8a46-d0f83df1d6c4" :answer/id #uuid "cc1e541a-a0b5-50e1-8a46-d0f83df1d6c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer/id #uuid "3f270f12-02ec-5549-8988-b03e5d2eae95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30fd4f57-3f0a-5ebc-aa07-bb84afb9cc7a" :answer/id #uuid "30fd4f57-3f0a-5ebc-aa07-bb84afb9cc7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)\n$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\n \n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$\n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198479 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186347802 :answer-field/correct 17592186345456 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-52ee3973-52bc-556a-8616-fb2686b552f2" :activity/id #uuid "52ee3973-52bc-556a-8616-fb2686b552f2" :activity/title "Differentials" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049789 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-cd2d74e5-abec-550d-9ecd-251a39a898c0" "step-36110881-4f06-5b62-a14a-640b5c8ecaec" "step-e1760d0a-7b68-5369-867b-8060b5dc8d02" "step-82baedff-1f18-5874-ab86-496b5c5150c2" "step-b86e363e-7ec4-59af-9dfb-ebd22f051bfc"] :activity/first-step "step-cd2d74e5-abec-550d-9ecd-251a39a898c0" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd2d74e5-abec-550d-9ecd-251a39a898c0" :step/id #uuid "cd2d74e5-abec-550d-9ecd-251a39a898c0" :step/content 17592186348265 :step/next "step-36110881-4f06-5b62-a14a-640b5c8ecaec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-36110881-4f06-5b62-a14a-640b5c8ecaec" :step/id #uuid "36110881-4f06-5b62-a14a-640b5c8ecaec" :step/content 17592186348271 :step/next "step-e1760d0a-7b68-5369-867b-8060b5dc8d02" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e1760d0a-7b68-5369-867b-8060b5dc8d02" :step/id #uuid "e1760d0a-7b68-5369-867b-8060b5dc8d02" :step/content 17592186348292 :step/next "step-82baedff-1f18-5874-ab86-496b5c5150c2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-82baedff-1f18-5874-ab86-496b5c5150c2" :step/id #uuid "82baedff-1f18-5874-ab86-496b5c5150c2" :step/content 17592186348293 :step/next "step-b86e363e-7ec4-59af-9dfb-ebd22f051bfc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b86e363e-7ec4-59af-9dfb-ebd22f051bfc" :step/id #uuid "b86e363e-7ec4-59af-9dfb-ebd22f051bfc" :step/content 17592186284581 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186052587 :answer-field/choices 17592186052588] [:db/retract 17592186052587 :answer-field/choices 17592186052589] [:db/retract 17592186052587 :answer-field/choices 17592186052591] {:db/id 17592186054709 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-307f44ca-a3a8-5008-a66b-8f406f11b7bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054354 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-2x|.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-2x| & =|2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054680 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4ad222fd-26eb-5815-83d7-f48153369720" "question-501f8db7-00d6-5d21-840c-caa3263e21e2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054576 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-18a|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|18a|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|18a| & = \\\\ |18\\cdot a| & = \\\\ |18|\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 18|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054681 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ff72e312-d04f-5312-b5f1-cf59d82ad7d7" "question-9b56ca18-7b32-5eb8-a22c-8e34fda01fb4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054577 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left|-\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{9}{5c}\\right|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{9}{5c}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|9|}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|5|\\cdot |c|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{5|c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-307f44ca-a3a8-5008-a66b-8f406f11b7bd" :question/id #uuid "307f44ca-a3a8-5008-a66b-8f406f11b7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-78841" :question/problem "$|-\\frac{2p}{r}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-\\frac{2p}{r}|=|\\frac{2p}{r}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c9ae3d3-ab29-5aa4-947b-af038ce31957"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c9ae3d3-ab29-5aa4-947b-af038ce31957" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9ae3d3-ab29-5aa4-947b-af038ce31957" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2b9d41c6-c253-5eb4-b807-5ad4ee3b7f4a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-afab7882-2117-5e0d-b040-644ea0e03ca9" "answer-a666697e-fce8-53a6-b75f-63cadf3413c1" "answer-d19e1936-45ef-55e9-ae74-25c19fd8c926" "answer-b4bea933-af75-5e83-802b-96308b1b05d2" "answer-2b9d41c6-c253-5eb4-b807-5ad4ee3b7f4a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afab7882-2117-5e0d-b040-644ea0e03ca9" :answer/id #uuid "afab7882-2117-5e0d-b040-644ea0e03ca9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a666697e-fce8-53a6-b75f-63cadf3413c1" :answer/id #uuid "a666697e-fce8-53a6-b75f-63cadf3413c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\frac{2p}{r}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d19e1936-45ef-55e9-ae74-25c19fd8c926" :answer/id #uuid "d19e1936-45ef-55e9-ae74-25c19fd8c926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{p}{2r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4bea933-af75-5e83-802b-96308b1b05d2" :answer/id #uuid "b4bea933-af75-5e83-802b-96308b1b05d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|2|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b9d41c6-c253-5eb4-b807-5ad4ee3b7f4a" :answer/id #uuid "2b9d41c6-c253-5eb4-b807-5ad4ee3b7f4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\frac{2p}{r}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055225 :question/problem "$|-\\frac{4z}{7}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-\\frac{4z}{7}|=|\\frac{4z}{7}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186052587 :answer-field/correct "answer-821b05c2-3969-5bd5-83f9-64e893309807" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1832f025-0d2f-5421-8261-378622d01a16" "answer-821b05c2-3969-5bd5-83f9-64e893309807" "answer-fb5318cf-1d2c-5097-a942-74e5ecc75615"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1832f025-0d2f-5421-8261-378622d01a16" :answer/id #uuid "1832f025-0d2f-5421-8261-378622d01a16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-|\\frac{4z}{7}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-821b05c2-3969-5bd5-83f9-64e893309807" :answer/id #uuid "821b05c2-3969-5bd5-83f9-64e893309807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\frac{4z}{7}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb5318cf-1d2c-5097-a942-74e5ecc75615" :answer/id #uuid "fb5318cf-1d2c-5097-a942-74e5ecc75615" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\frac{4}{7}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4ad222fd-26eb-5815-83d7-f48153369720" :question/id #uuid "4ad222fd-26eb-5815-83d7-f48153369720" :question/math-academy-id "q-111983" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-9a|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|9a|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|9a| & = \\\\ |9\\cdot a| & = \\\\ |9|\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 9\\cdot |a| & = \\\\ 9|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e0e3c8da-c569-5435-b9a3-10772d1de6ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0e3c8da-c569-5435-b9a3-10772d1de6ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e3c8da-c569-5435-b9a3-10772d1de6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b30999cc-c633-58f2-83be-6ced52d8ffdb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08f20868-6a4d-50d9-a8bc-710ffae4cb97" "answer-85811a93-a7cf-52b7-942f-22f7a24348c2" "answer-af905c3b-1939-597d-9d71-3aef9f1ab184" "answer-da00413d-224c-5153-8766-d44fa73b23d3" "answer-b30999cc-c633-58f2-83be-6ced52d8ffdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08f20868-6a4d-50d9-a8bc-710ffae4cb97" :answer/id #uuid "08f20868-6a4d-50d9-a8bc-710ffae4cb97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85811a93-a7cf-52b7-942f-22f7a24348c2" :answer/id #uuid "85811a93-a7cf-52b7-942f-22f7a24348c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9|a|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af905c3b-1939-597d-9d71-3aef9f1ab184" :answer/id #uuid "af905c3b-1939-597d-9d71-3aef9f1ab184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da00413d-224c-5153-8766-d44fa73b23d3" :answer/id #uuid "da00413d-224c-5153-8766-d44fa73b23d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b30999cc-c633-58f2-83be-6ced52d8ffdb" :answer/id #uuid "b30999cc-c633-58f2-83be-6ced52d8ffdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9|a|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-501f8db7-00d6-5d21-840c-caa3263e21e2" :question/id #uuid "501f8db7-00d6-5d21-840c-caa3263e21e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-111990" :question/problem "$|14y|=$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|14y| & = \\\\ |14\\cdot y| & = \\\\ |14|\\cdot |y| & = \\\\ 14\\cdot |y| & = \\\\ 14|y|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4836ca62-9f61-52ac-8477-487dc8437b03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4836ca62-9f61-52ac-8477-487dc8437b03" :answer-field/id #uuid "4836ca62-9f61-52ac-8477-487dc8437b03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-612c8141-f91e-56a2-8f54-b2d7860bf1ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0fb66c0-edd9-52f1-a4df-e490eaa76a8c" "answer-3bf7530f-ac07-5a78-8244-a27abfb60d89" "answer-478a886a-0b12-5dbe-9945-1bc8a7e8980d" "answer-612c8141-f91e-56a2-8f54-b2d7860bf1ef" "answer-d68d5049-4a5a-5ab4-bf44-1c8f71e81a8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0fb66c0-edd9-52f1-a4df-e490eaa76a8c" :answer/id #uuid "e0fb66c0-edd9-52f1-a4df-e490eaa76a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bf7530f-ac07-5a78-8244-a27abfb60d89" :answer/id #uuid "3bf7530f-ac07-5a78-8244-a27abfb60d89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14|y|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-478a886a-0b12-5dbe-9945-1bc8a7e8980d" :answer/id #uuid "478a886a-0b12-5dbe-9945-1bc8a7e8980d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-612c8141-f91e-56a2-8f54-b2d7860bf1ef" :answer/id #uuid "612c8141-f91e-56a2-8f54-b2d7860bf1ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14|y|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d68d5049-4a5a-5ab4-bf44-1c8f71e81a8a" :answer/id #uuid "d68d5049-4a5a-5ab4-bf44-1c8f71e81a8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff72e312-d04f-5312-b5f1-cf59d82ad7d7" :question/id #uuid "ff72e312-d04f-5312-b5f1-cf59d82ad7d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78933" :question/problem "$|-\\frac{5p}{2}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{5p}{2}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{5p}{2}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|5p|}{|2|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5\\cdot p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{|5|\\cdot |p|}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5|p|}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7334cd7-da00-5f5a-a61d-43f7925f796c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7334cd7-da00-5f5a-a61d-43f7925f796c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7334cd7-da00-5f5a-a61d-43f7925f796c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14df1b8b-c09f-5f4f-8912-bce12aef73f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1bca8841-c608-5df1-a39f-72e10b74000f" "answer-ef5a1d4e-4c65-5a3d-9293-71fda013cbca" "answer-e25f1198-58ef-5316-a79a-76a71cb9dca8" "answer-55caa650-df35-59bd-bd90-4e9fd14e444a" "answer-14df1b8b-c09f-5f4f-8912-bce12aef73f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bca8841-c608-5df1-a39f-72e10b74000f" :answer/id #uuid "1bca8841-c608-5df1-a39f-72e10b74000f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5|p|}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef5a1d4e-4c65-5a3d-9293-71fda013cbca" :answer/id #uuid "ef5a1d4e-4c65-5a3d-9293-71fda013cbca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e25f1198-58ef-5316-a79a-76a71cb9dca8" :answer/id #uuid "e25f1198-58ef-5316-a79a-76a71cb9dca8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55caa650-df35-59bd-bd90-4e9fd14e444a" :answer/id #uuid "55caa650-df35-59bd-bd90-4e9fd14e444a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5p}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14df1b8b-c09f-5f4f-8912-bce12aef73f8" :answer/id #uuid "14df1b8b-c09f-5f4f-8912-bce12aef73f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5|p|}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b56ca18-7b32-5eb8-a22c-8e34fda01fb4" :question/id #uuid "9b56ca18-7b32-5eb8-a22c-8e34fda01fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78907" :question/problem "$|-\\frac{8}{7z}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{8}{7z}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the division, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{8}{7z}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|8|}{|7z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7\\cdot z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{|7|\\cdot |z|} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{7|z|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cded725a-dfe2-53ba-903c-8e4d5a08f11d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cded725a-dfe2-53ba-903c-8e4d5a08f11d" :answer-field/id #uuid "cded725a-dfe2-53ba-903c-8e4d5a08f11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b21cf601-c80a-571f-be86-fe9a0465fc46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b0fa393-9bee-571c-812f-725f1e012ed7" "answer-18dcdf6a-ecae-52ab-8293-7ebd8c69e629" "answer-f842a0ab-0c8a-584b-8c0f-87f8286b4a05" "answer-b21cf601-c80a-571f-be86-fe9a0465fc46" "answer-2a94091a-8723-5ac9-ab16-627223c2b062"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b0fa393-9bee-571c-812f-725f1e012ed7" :answer/id #uuid "7b0fa393-9bee-571c-812f-725f1e012ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8z}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18dcdf6a-ecae-52ab-8293-7ebd8c69e629" :answer/id #uuid "18dcdf6a-ecae-52ab-8293-7ebd8c69e629" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{7|z|}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f842a0ab-0c8a-584b-8c0f-87f8286b4a05" :answer/id #uuid "f842a0ab-0c8a-584b-8c0f-87f8286b4a05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8z}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b21cf601-c80a-571f-be86-fe9a0465fc46" :answer/id #uuid "b21cf601-c80a-571f-be86-fe9a0465fc46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7|z|}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a94091a-8723-5ac9-ab16-627223c2b062" :answer/id #uuid "2a94091a-8723-5ac9-ab16-627223c2b062" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055949 :tutorial/content "Recall that the absolute value of a number represents the distance of that number from the origin.\n\nLet's take a look at the number line below.\n\n![](images/e2/e20db43f722fc4db42fc845ab20a7eef2c06437cc1d2246e0aed47dc01107aba.png)\n\nSince $-3$ and $3$ are at the same distance from the origin, we have\n\n$$\n|-3| = |3| = 3.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any real number $a,$\n\n$$\n|-a| = |a|.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify expressions containing absolute value. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055996 :tutorial/content "The absolute value operation is **distributive with respect to multiplication.** This means that the absolute value of a product equals the product of the absolute values.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n|(-6) \\cdot 2|\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this expression in two separate ways:\n\n- The first is to find the absolute value of the product. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(-6)\\cdot 2| & =|-12| \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second is to find the product of the absolute values. In doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|(-6)\\cdot 2| & =|-6|\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general\n\n$$\n|a \\cdot b| = |a|\\cdot |b|.\n$$\n\nThe absolute value operation is also distributive with respect to division. In general,\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054923 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281374 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198621 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281379 :question/problem "![](images/57/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186153490 :answer-field/choices 17592186153491] [:db/retract 17592186153490 :answer-field/choices 17592186153492] [:db/retract 17592186153490 :answer-field/choices 17592186153493] [:db/retract 17592186153490 :answer-field/choices 17592186153494] [:db/retract 17592186153490 :answer-field/choices 17592186153495] {:db/id 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" :question/id #uuid "c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102684" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cad98c5-f0af-530e-9545-f63d666cd3ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cad98c5-f0af-530e-9545-f63d666cd3ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cad98c5-f0af-530e-9545-f63d666cd3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c00142a0-9805-5734-8860-3bf5256f853f" "answer-e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" "answer-93574e00-8aff-52c2-8fef-8d24d76bb02a" "answer-aee9d420-4960-50a5-922a-c43137c76d7d" "answer-bc4a4294-c877-56f6-bfd0-3109ee79c13c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c00142a0-9805-5734-8860-3bf5256f853f" :answer/id #uuid "c00142a0-9805-5734-8860-3bf5256f853f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer/id #uuid "e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93574e00-8aff-52c2-8fef-8d24d76bb02a" :answer/id #uuid "93574e00-8aff-52c2-8fef-8d24d76bb02a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aee9d420-4960-50a5-922a-c43137c76d7d" :answer/id #uuid "aee9d420-4960-50a5-922a-c43137c76d7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc4a4294-c877-56f6-bfd0-3109ee79c13c" :answer/id #uuid "bc4a4294-c877-56f6-bfd0-3109ee79c13c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157541 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-2x{y}^{3} & x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x{y}^{3})=-2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x{y}^{3})=1-6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{y}^{3} & 1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 1-6x{y}^{2} & -6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,0).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153490 :answer-field/correct "answer-8cb3995c-cc62-5136-82b2-472b0cb824be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fca28ad-f59e-5a0a-bb4c-0662386d1e94" "answer-dc10b03f-56c5-527c-94f1-47709864755a" "answer-d0ef3863-4e52-56dd-9984-66598e15e8ff" "answer-3075a971-db98-5b66-a3d1-9af12fdf7be7" "answer-8cb3995c-cc62-5136-82b2-472b0cb824be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fca28ad-f59e-5a0a-bb4c-0662386d1e94" :answer/id #uuid "2fca28ad-f59e-5a0a-bb4c-0662386d1e94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc10b03f-56c5-527c-94f1-47709864755a" :answer/id #uuid "dc10b03f-56c5-527c-94f1-47709864755a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0ef3863-4e52-56dd-9984-66598e15e8ff" :answer/id #uuid "d0ef3863-4e52-56dd-9984-66598e15e8ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3075a971-db98-5b66-a3d1-9af12fdf7be7" :answer/id #uuid "3075a971-db98-5b66-a3d1-9af12fdf7be7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cb3995c-cc62-5136-82b2-472b0cb824be" :answer/id #uuid "8cb3995c-cc62-5136-82b2-472b0cb824be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0d220057-0079-57e6-bb5c-eb3461a91e72" :activity/id #uuid "0d220057-0079-57e6-bb5c-eb3461a91e72" :activity/title "The Second Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048859 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-4e33b2ed-2bc9-51ba-9064-eab461a12ff8" "step-63ea7e02-9f7a-5a09-b718-d06795e4cb97" "step-098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" "step-a882a718-46ae-5afd-bb1b-85e1be5639e2" "step-66302a80-8659-5359-be45-331fa2372867"] :activity/first-step "step-4e33b2ed-2bc9-51ba-9064-eab461a12ff8" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4e33b2ed-2bc9-51ba-9064-eab461a12ff8" :step/id #uuid "4e33b2ed-2bc9-51ba-9064-eab461a12ff8" :step/content 17592186351196 :step/next "step-63ea7e02-9f7a-5a09-b718-d06795e4cb97" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-63ea7e02-9f7a-5a09-b718-d06795e4cb97" :step/id #uuid "63ea7e02-9f7a-5a09-b718-d06795e4cb97" :step/content "question-c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" :step/next "step-098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" :step/id #uuid "098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" :step/content 17592186237927 :step/next "step-a882a718-46ae-5afd-bb1b-85e1be5639e2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a882a718-46ae-5afd-bb1b-85e1be5639e2" :step/id #uuid "a882a718-46ae-5afd-bb1b-85e1be5639e2" :step/content 17592186430448 :step/next "step-66302a80-8659-5359-be45-331fa2372867" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-66302a80-8659-5359-be45-331fa2372867" :step/id #uuid "66302a80-8659-5359-be45-331fa2372867" :step/content 17592186157541 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186062113 :answer-field/choices 17592186062114] [:db/retract 17592186062113 :answer-field/choices 17592186062115] [:db/retract 17592186062113 :answer-field/choices 17592186062116] [:db/retract 17592186062113 :answer-field/choices 17592186062117] [:db/retract 17592186062113 :answer-field/choices 17592186062118] {:db/id 17592186064241 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f29a61b-d59f-5f7b-8b19-ba0d9192d12c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063988 :question/problem "Expand and simplify $(y + 5)(y - 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(y + 5)(y - 2) &= \\\n\t\ty(y-2) + 5(y-2) &=\\\n\t\ty^2-2y +5y-10 &=\\\n\t\ty^2 + 3y - 10\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064242 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-04f1ace1-c4c7-5004-a410-92cc0ba374dc" "question-84908780-ec57-5aef-8661-1e98636ea221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063989 :question/problem "Expand and simplify $(2n - 1)(3n - 6).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2n - 1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t2n(3n - 6) + (-1)(3n - 6) &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 12n - 3n + 6 &= \\\n\t\t6n^2 - 15n + 6\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064243 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6e37bd4e-5f8a-5417-aabd-8b7d37d96ee7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063990 :question/problem "Expand $(p^2-q^2)(2p+3q).$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}(2p+3q)+(-{q}^{2})(2p+3q) & = \\\\ 2{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}q-2p{q}^{2}-3{q}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064676 :question/problem "$(q+6)(q+2)=$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q+6)(q+2) & = \\\\ q(q+2)+6(q+2) & = \\\\ {q}^{2}+2q+6q+12 & = \\\\ {q}^{2}+8q+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062113 :answer-field/correct "answer-55a60124-b85f-5634-bcc9-5b892c5317c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b5aa05e-a354-5093-8e3c-d00252beb5de" "answer-939e84cc-3e36-5e09-a1b0-85393d657a76" "answer-1d2198ba-923e-5056-b8e4-6d9278cbada9" "answer-9c32ca25-8e1f-5a20-8a20-18245e0893d0" "answer-55a60124-b85f-5634-bcc9-5b892c5317c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b5aa05e-a354-5093-8e3c-d00252beb5de" :answer/id #uuid "6b5aa05e-a354-5093-8e3c-d00252beb5de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9q+12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-939e84cc-3e36-5e0